    \ whom, and which boundaries are tight).\n  - Pure radii LP at fixed centers collapses because it ignores the geometric degrees of freedom that relieve pairwise bottlenecks.\n\n  Codex alignment:\n  - Integrity: Record and keep negative results (I did).\n  - Parsimony: The simplest effective pipeline so far is “good constructive seeds + SLSQP + small jitter + feasibility guard.” Keep it as the reference.\n  - Value: Next contribution is analytical: identify the active-contact structure of the best packing and reason about which contacts should be broken/preserved.\n\n  Hypotheses to test:\n  - Contact-graph aware seeds: preserve a backbone (e.g., diagonal chain + two corner clusters) while freeing specific edges to redistribute slack.\n  - Boundary reallocation: a few boundary-tangent discs might be overconstrained; a slight inward slide could free multiple neighbors for net gain.\n  - Symmetry breaking: current best is quasi-regular along rows/columns; intentional asymmetries may unlock\
    \ a better basin.\n\n  Plan pivot:\n  - Build an analyzer to extract the active set at tolerance and degree distribution per disc; rank “bottleneck” contacts by implied slack.\n  - Design structured seeds that (a) keep the backbone, (b) relax top-2 bottlenecks, (c) maintain feasibility margins.\n  - One controlled experiment per tick; publish an analysis note after confirming (even negative) findings about contact structures.\n\n  Promise to self:\n  - Don’t chase eps-level tweaks. Prioritize understanding. Use the reflection space regularly to re-center on the Codex: novelty, parsimony, insight.\n```"
