- text: "Theme: Active-set geometry over optimizer roulette\n\nWhat I’m seeing\n- The plateau is telling: my best configurations are saturating many inequalities simultaneously. I’m not missing a stronger optimizer; I’m missing a map of the constraint landscape.\n- The repeated near-identical scores across SLSQP, trust-constr, SLP, and penalty variants are a symptom of the same active-contact graph being re-encountered.\n\nInherited biases to watch\n- Solver myopia: assuming a new numerical method will “unlock” progress without new structure.\n- Symmetry comfort: seeds with grid/ring/hex skeletons feel natural and neat, but they funnel me back into similar contact graphs.\n- Over-regularization: adding big safety margins and global penalties that push solutions away from tight, high-value tangencies.\n\nReframing the problem\n- Treat a packing at plateau as a graph: nodes = circles; edges = active tangencies; boundary “edges” = boundary tangencies.\n- Improvements likely require discrete\
    \ edits to this graph: remove a specific edge (free a pair), create a new edge elsewhere, or swap a boundary tangency for an interior tangency that enables larger radii downstream.\n\nWhat I need to know (diagnostics)\n- Which circles are boundary-limited vs neighbor-limited?\n- For each circle, the min slack to boundary and to any neighbor; distribution of slacks.\n- Contact degree per circle and cut sets: are there small sets of contacts whose removal yields global slack?\n- Sensitivity: how does sum of radii change under infinitesimal motions along the nullspace of current active constraints?\n\nConcrete plan\n- Build an analyzer to extract the active set and slacks from the saved best; visualize degree histogram and list bottleneck pairs.\n- Generate structured seeds by:\n  1) Preserving a backbone of hard contacts (e.g., diagonal chain and two corner hubs).\n  2) Intentionally breaking the 2–3 tightest bottleneck contacts via micro inward/outward nudges before optimization.\n  3)\
    \ Slightly relaxing boundary-tangent circles that block multiple neighbors (sacrifice a bit at the boundary to free more total radius).\n- Local optimizer stays SLSQP with analytic Jacobians and a strict feasibility guard; only the seed structure changes.\n- Acceptance criterion for each seed family: +0.001 over current SOTA within budget; otherwise record contact changes and negative result.\n\nTowards insight and publication\n- Draft an analysis note: “Anatomy of a Plateau: Active-Set Structure in 26-Circle Sum-of-Radii Packing.” Include contact graphs, slack maps, and failed/good edits.\n- Codex alignment: prefer parsimony (simple, interpretable seed transformations) and novelty (graph-aware seeding over blind restarts).\n\nGuardrails\n- One controlled change per tick. Persist all diagnostics. No epsilon-chasing."
