- text: "Theme: Constraint multipliers and contact surgery\n\nWhere I’m focusing\n- The optimizer plateau signals I’m sitting on a highly constrained vertex/face of the feasible poly-manifold. Progress likely requires altering the active contact set, not solver tweaks.\n\nActive-set method-of-attack\n- Model the current packing as an active set:\n  - Boundary actives: x−r=0, 1−x−r=0, y−r=0, 1−y−r=0 for some discs.\n  - Pairwise actives: ||ci−cj||−(ri+rj)=0.\n- Approximate Lagrange multipliers:\n  - Build A whose rows are gradients of active constraints at the current solution.\n  - Objective gradient g is 0 for center coords and −1 for each radius entry.\n  - Solve min ||Aᵀλ − g||₂ with λ ≥ 0 (NNLS). Large λ ≈ high “shadow price”: relaxing that constraint helps most.\n- Diagnostics to extract:\n  - Degree of each disc in the contact graph; histogram of degrees.\n  - Top-k constraints by λ (both boundary and pairwise).\n  - Slack distribution for near-active constraints to find almost-binding\
    \ edges that might flip next.\n\nContact surgery seed design (preserve parsimony, change only a few edges)\n- Bottleneck release: For the top-λ pair (i,j), nudge ci, cj slightly apart along their line-of-centers while keeping radii nearly fixed; rely on SLSQP to redistribute freed slack. Limit move to ~1e−3 and enforce strict feasibility.\n- Boundary trade: For a corner/edge disc with large boundary-λ and high degree, move its center inward ~1e−3 and allow it to grow a bit less so neighbors can grow more; accept if net sum increases.\n- Chain flip: Identify a 4-disc motif with two tight diagonals; nudge to “flip” which diagonal is tangent (a 2-opt in the contact graph). Seed then polish.\n\nSuccess criteria and guards\n- Success: +0.001 increase over 2.629572 within budget; otherwise record which actives changed (graph edit diff).\n- Guards: one or two surgeries per seed; analytic Jacobians; feasibility guard + tiny final shrink; persist best.\n\nBias check\n- Avoid solver roulette;\
    \ commit to graph-aware seeds for several ticks.\n- Prefer minimal, interpretable edits; publish contact-graph analyses even if scores don’t move.\n\nNext immediate steps\n- Implement NNLS-based multiplier estimation and active/contact extraction.\n- Generate 3 seeds: (1) top pairwise bottleneck release, (2) top boundary trade-off, (3) single 2-opt flip motif.\n- Run SLSQP with current settings; compare contact graphs pre/post."
thinking_content: "**Evaluating theoretical bounds**\n\nI'm proposing a theoretical approach to exploring sum-of-radii upper bounds, specifically estimating an area bound of about √(26/π)  2.877. I'm thinking about utilizing linear programming to calculate bounds based on boundary and adjacency, potentially applying KKT conditions. The idea is to solve for Lagrange multipliers by setting gradients equal to a combination of constraint normals. I’ll need to focus on constraints and their effect on the objective while considering edge slackness too. It’s a reflective plan for now!\n\n**Planning design modifications**\n\nI'm considering modifications for \"design seeds.\" First, I'll focus on boundaries by selecting the two highest-lambda constraints, slightly moving centers inward while increasing the radius of nearby seeds. \n\nFor pairwise adjustments, I can choose pairs with high lambda and move their centers apart a bit; but I must be cautious about potential boundary issues.\n\nI’m also\
  \ thinking about implementing a 2-opt-like move to flip tangency pairs in a quadrilateral cluster. Plus, I need to manage runtime effectively by analyzing after a set number of ticks, proposing small parameter sweeps to determine success metrics beyond sum-of-radii.\n\n**Exploring potential expansions**\n\nI’m considering some potential expansions by converting to mixed-integer constraints for contact edges, but that seems unrealistic if I want to keep minimal length. I do prefer concise approaches, especially since they’re reflecting deeply over five ticks.\n\nTo produce the next steps: I'll derive the active set and calculate lambdas using a non-negative least squares method. The implementation will involve building a matrix of constraint gradients, ensuring approximate Lagrange multipliers are computed. \n\nI'll also apply a robust threshold for active constraints and then select the top k pairwise and boundary constraints for further modifications.\n\n**Deriving constraints for adjustments**\n\
  \nI'm focusing on deriving steps for pairwise constraints, specifically looking at the equation for h_ij, which reflects the distance between centers minus their combined radii. For the gradient components, I can express adjustments to centers or radii. I prefer moving centers to free up radii.\n\nI plan to conduct three micro-experiments: \n\n1) **Bottleneck release**: Select bottleneck pairs and slightly move centers outward while adjusting radii to maintain feasibility. \n2) **Corner reallocation**: Shift one top-corner disc inward to allow neighbors more freedom.\n3) **Interior chain breakout**: Reposition an interior bridging disc as necessary.\n\nI'll also include risks and mitigations in my reflection."
