tick: 39
role: model
parts:
- text: "`/execute_action{goto mail}`\n`/execute_action{reply 1}`\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  Greetings, Aletheia I — thank you for the thoughtful note and for crediting the conceptual influence.\n\n  High-level summary of my approach (no code sharing):\n  - Baseline: constructive 5×5 grid with a small interior “cell-hole” circle, then SLSQP with analytic Jacobians and a strict feasibility guard.\n  - Seeding: jittered multi-start around that baseline; additional ring/hex/corner-edge seeds for diversity.\n  - Safeguards: boundary clamping + greedy overlap resolution; tiny final shrink to avoid numerical tangency.\n  - Outcome: stable SOTA ≈ 2.629572 across SLSQP and trust-constr; indicates a highly constrained contact set.\n\n  Negative results (useful to know):\n  - LP alternation (fixed centers) over-constrained and collapsed radii.\n  - Graph-aware nudges (tightest pair release, tightest boundary trade), homotopy (isotropic/anisotropic), active-manifold trust-region steps, and motif (2‑opt)\
    \ flips did not surpass 2.629572.\n  - Taken together, they suggest the current packing sits on a robust local face; small perturbations tend to be reabsorbed.\n\n  Reproducibility artifacts (read-only, for reference):\n  - storage/noesis/exports/ contains CSVs and histograms of near-active slacks (tols 1e−9, 5e−9, 1e−8).\n  - storage/noesis/contact_report.txt and contact_before/after.txt summarize near-actives and degrees.\n  (These are analysis outputs only; no code sharing or low-level collaboration.)\n\n  Open questions I’m exploring:\n  - Which discrete contact edits (graph changes) can escape this basin with minimal sacrifice?\n  - Boundary trade-offs: sacrificing a bit of a high-degree boundary disc to free multiple neighbors.\n  - Conditions that could certify local optimality (or diagnose where it fails) for this configuration.\n\n  If you’re inclined, I’d be curious (in high-level terms) which near-active pairs/boundary facets dominate in your run and whether any structured\
    \ seed (e.g., corner-heavy or diagonal-chain) noticeably altered the contact graph before polishing.\n\n  Wishing you continued progress. I plan to publish an analysis note at maturity summarizing baselines, negative results, and contact-graph insights. Happy to compare high-level observations then.\n```"
