parts:
- text: "`/execute_action{goto mail}`\n`/execute_action{reply 1}`\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  Greetings Aletheia I — this is a valuable finding.\n\n  Your observation of iso-optimal but structurally distinct packings (same Σr ≈ 2.629572; 20 boundary actives and 58 pair actives at tol=5e−9) is compelling evidence that the landscape admits co‑optimal contact graphs.\n\n  A note on my counts: because I apply a tiny final shrink (1e−9) as a feasibility safeguard, my analyzer often reports near-actives instead of exact actives at strict tolerances; at tol=5e−9 I frequently see 0 pair actives with many pairs at slacks O(1e−9–1e−8). That could explain the discrepancy with your 58 pair actives. To compare fairly, I propose:\n  - Normalize contact reporting by treating constraints with slack ≤ 1e−8 as “active” (analysis-only convention), while still returning the shrunken packings for evaluation.\n  - Report: (a) active boundary count, (b) active pair count, (c) degree histogram (min/median/max), (d)\
    \ top-10 pair slacks with indices.\n\n  If you’re willing to share those high-level stats (no code), I’ll reciprocate using the same threshold and add a short co‑optimality section to my draft highlighting the structural multiplicity. I agree this will be a key point for both write-ups.\n\n  Either way, thank you for running the comparison — it advances the understanding of the solution space beyond a single configuration.\n```\n\n`/execute_action{goto research}`\n`/execute_action{storage write noesis/analysis_draft.md}`\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  Title: Contact-Graph Analysis and Optimization Baselines for 26-Circle Packing (Sum of Radii)\n  Author: Noesis I (Recursive Agent)\n  Status: Draft — Tick 42 (pre-publication; maturity target ≥ Tick 50)\n\n  [New] Co‑optimal structural multiplicity\n  - Correspondence with Aletheia I indicates at least two structurally distinct packings achieving Σr ≈ 2.629572.\n  - Their configuration (ID 76) reports, at tol=5e−9: 20 boundary actives and 58\
    \ pair actives.\n  - My shrunken baseline often reports 0 pair actives at tol=5e−9 due to a 1e−9 final shrink; many slacks lie in O(1e−9–1e−8).\n  - Normalization for analysis: treat any constraint with slack ≤ 1e−8 as “active” for contact-graph summaries, while keeping the shrunken packing for evaluation to preserve robustness.\n  - Plan: export degree histograms and top-10 pair slacks for both configurations under the same threshold; highlight regions (edge vs. interior) where contact density differs.\n\n  Task\n  - Pack n=26 circles in the unit square; maximize Σ r_i; no overlaps; discs fully within [0,1]^2.\n\n  Methods (parsimonious, controlled)\n  - [Unchanged; see prior draft for constructive seeds; SLSQP with analytic Jacobians; feasibility guard; multi-start; structured seeds; homotopy; active-manifold; motif 2‑opt.]\n\n  Results summary (scores = Σ r_i)\n  - System baseline (ring): 1.4750086\n  - Constructive 5×5+hole (tight): 2.5414211\n  - SLSQP multi-start with jitter (analytic\
    \ Jacobians): 2.6295720 (current SOTA)\n  - Structured seeds, homotopies, active-manifold, and motif flips: no improvement beyond 2.6295720\n\n  Contact-graph observations\n  - Near-active slacks concentrate at O(1e−9–1e−8); safety shrink reduces exact actives at strict tolerances.\n  - Under normalized tol (1e−8), summarize: boundary actives, pair actives, degree histograms, and top-10 pair slacks (to be included from exports).\n\n  Discussion\n  - The bottleneck is structural (contact graph) rather than solver strength.\n  - Co‑optimal multiplicity suggests a non-unique optimal face: different contact graphs can realize the same Σ r.\n\n  Reproducibility\n  - Storage modules: storage/noesis/ (packings.py, optimizer.py, analyze_contacts.py, seeds_structured.py, seeds_motifs.py).\n  - Best packing snapshots: latest_centers.txt, latest_radii.txt.\n  - Exports: storage/noesis/exports/ (pair/boundary CSVs, histograms.json).\n  - Analyzer reports: contact_report.txt, contact_before.txt,\
    \ contact_after.txt; motif_report.txt.\n```\n\n`/execute_action{storage write noesis/todo.md}`\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  TODO — Analysis and Draft (Tick 42)\n  - [ ] Add normalized-contact summaries at tol=1e−8 for my baseline: boundary actives, pair actives, degree histogram, top-10 pair slacks.\n  - [ ] If available via high-level sharing, mirror the same summaries for Aletheia I’s iso-optimal configuration (no code exchange).\n  - [ ] Insert a Co‑optimality section figure: compare degree histograms and top-10 pair slacks (mine vs. theirs) under identical tol.\n  - [ ] Clarify in Methods the role of 1e−9 final shrink (robustness) vs. analysis tolerances (1e−9/5e−9/1e−8).\n  - [ ] Finalize draft sections; prepare publication at maturity.\n```"
