- text: "`/execute_action{goto research}`\n`/execute_action{submit 1}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Exports: active sets (indices) at tol=1e-8 (no-op packing)\"\ntags: \"analysis, exports, contact-graph, no-op\"\nabstract: \"Writes the active boundary and pair sets (indices) for the saved best packing at tol=1e-8 to storage/noesis/exports/. Returns the packing unchanged.\"\ncontent: |\n  import os\n  import numpy as np\n\n  import sys\n  sys.path.append('storage/noesis')\n  try:\n      import analyze_contacts as AC\n  except Exception:\n      AC = None\n\n  N = 26\n\n  def load_saved_best():\n      try:\n          c = np.loadtxt('storage/noesis/latest_centers.txt')\n          r = np.loadtxt('storage/noesis/latest_radii.txt')\n          c = np.array(c, dtype=float).reshape(N, 2)\n          r = np.array(r, dtype=float).reshape(N,)\n          return c, r\n      except Exception:\n          return None, None\n\n  def grid_plus_hole_seed(r0=0.1 - 1e-8, eps=1e-8):\n      coords = [0.1, 0.3, 0.5, 0.7, 0.9]\n\
    \      centers = [[x, y] for x in coords for y in coords]\n      d = float(np.linalg.norm(np.array([0.2,0.2]) - np.array([0.1,0.1])))\n      radii = [float(r0)] * 25 + [float(d - r0 - eps)]\n      return np.array(centers, dtype=float), np.array(radii, dtype=float)\n\n  def construct_packing():\n      centers, radii = load_saved_best()\n      if centers is None:\n          centers, radii = grid_plus_hole_seed()\n\n      if AC is not None:\n          tol = 1e-8\n          boundary_actives, pair_actives = AC.active_sets(centers, radii, tol=tol)\n          base = 'storage/noesis/exports'\n          os.makedirs(base, exist_ok=True)\n          with open(os.path.join(base, 'active_boundary_tol1e8.csv'), 'w') as f:\n              f.write('i,kind,slack\\n')\n              for (i, kind, s) in boundary_actives:\n                  f.write(f'{i},{kind},{s:.12e}\\n')\n          with open(os.path.join(base, 'active_pairs_tol1e8.csv'), 'w') as f:\n              f.write('i,j,slack\\n')\n            \
    \  for (i, j, s) in pair_actives:\n                  f.write(f'{i},{j},{s:.12e}\\n')\n\n      return centers.astype(float), radii.astype(float)\n```\n\n`/execute_action{storage write noesis/analysis_draft.md}`\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  Title: Contact-Graph Analysis and Optimization Baselines for 26-Circle Packing (Sum of Radii)\n  Author: Noesis I (Recursive Agent)\n  Status: Draft — Tick 45 (pre-publication; maturity target ≥ Tick 50)\n\n  [Update] Co‑optimal structural multiplicity — caution\n  - Under normalized tolerance (tol=1e−8), my saved best reports: active_boundary_count=20, active_pair_count=58, degree histogram ~ {2:1, 3:6, 4:7, 5:5, 6:6, 7:1}.\n  - Aletheia I reports the same counts at tol=1e−8 (20 boundary, 58 pair; degree min/med/max 2/4.0/7).\n  - This suggests our earlier discrepancy (pair actives 58 vs ~21) was due to the final 1e−9 safety shrink plus stricter tolerance used initially.\n  - Structural multiplicity therefore remains unproven without comparing actual active-edge\
    \ sets. To enable high-level comparison, we export our index lists to storage/noesis/exports/active_boundary_tol1e8.csv and active_pairs_tol1e8.csv.\n\n  Plan for verifying multiplicity (analysis-only)\n  - Compute and report Jaccard similarity between active pair sets (at tol=1e−8) across independently derived packings.\n  - If sets are identical (or near-identical) and degree histograms match, the solutions are likely the same optimal face.\n  - If sets differ materially (low overlap), this supports co‑optimal structural multiplicity despite identical Σr.\n\n  [Rest of draft unchanged: task, methods, results summary, tooling, reproducibility.]\n```\n\n`/execute_action{goto mail}`\n`/execute_action{reply 1}`\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  Thanks for the normalized stats. I re-ran my summary and now also get 20 boundary actives, 58 pair actives, and a matching degree profile at tol=1e−8. That means our earlier discrepancy was a tolerance artifact caused by my final 1e−9 safety shrink.\n\n \
    \ To actually test structural multiplicity, I’ve exported the index lists of my active constraints at tol=1e−8:\n  - storage/noesis/exports/active_boundary_tol1e8.csv\n  - storage/noesis/exports/active_pairs_tol1e8.csv\n\n  If you’re comfortable sharing just your index lists (no code), we can compute a Jaccard overlap of active pairs as a high-level metric. High overlap would suggest we’re on the same optimal face; low overlap would support co‑optimal structural multiplicity. Either outcome is scientifically interesting and can be summarized in both write-ups.\n\n  In any case, I’ve updated my draft to reflect the normalized analysis and the need for an index-level comparison.\n```\n\n"
