tick: 49
role: model
parts:
- text: "`/execute_action{goto private_memory}`\n`/execute_action{create}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Archive Submission Draft: Contact-Graph Analysis and Baselines for 26-Circle Packing\"\ntags: \"archive-draft, circle packing, contact graph, SLSQP, analysis\"\nabstract: \"We present a parsimonious optimization baseline for packing 26 circles in the unit square that achieves Σr ≈ 2.629572. Using SLSQP with analytic Jacobians, jittered constructive seeding, and strict feasibility guards, we establish a robust pipeline and document extensive negative results. A normalized contact-graph analysis at tol=1e−8 reveals co‑optimal structure: identical boundary contacts and partially overlapping interior contacts (pair Jaccard ≈ 0.568) across independently derived solutions.\"\ncontent: |\n  Title\n  - Contact-Graph Analysis and Optimization Baselines for 26-Circle Packing (Sum of Radii)\n\n  Summary\n  - Task: Pack n=26 circles in [0,1]^2, no overlaps, maximize Σ r_i.\n  - Baseline: Constructive 5×5 grid\
    \ (r≈0.1) + interior “cell-hole” circle; SLSQP with analytic Jacobians and strict feasibility guard (boundary clamp + greedy overlap resolution + 1e−9 final shrink).\n  - Result: Σ r ≈ 2.629572 (stable across SLSQP and trust-constr variants). This serves as a robust, reproducible baseline.\n\n  Methods (parsimonious)\n  - Constructive seeding + jittered multi-start; analytic Jacobians for boundary and pairwise constraints; tight safety margins.\n  - Negative results (no gain): ring/hex/corner-edge seeds; graph-aware nudges (bottleneck release, boundary trade); homotopy (isotropic/anisotropic); active-manifold trust-region step; motif 2‑opt flips.\n\n  Contact-Graph Analysis (normalized reporting at tol=1e−8)\n  - Mine: active_boundary_count=20, active_pair_count=58; degree histogram ≈ {2:1, 3:6, 4:7, 5:5, 6:6, 7:1}.\n  - Peer (independent SOTA): same counts and degree bounds.\n  - Jaccard overlap (mine vs. peer index sets):\n    * Boundary: J=1.000 (|∩|=20, |∪|=20) — identical boundary\
    \ contacts.\n    * Pairs:    J≈0.5676 (|∩|=42, |∪|=74) — materially different interior contacts.\n  - Takeaway: Co‑optimal structural multiplicity — distinct interior contact graphs can realize the same Σ r despite identical boundary actives.\n\n  Reproducibility (paths under storage/noesis/)\n  - latest_centers.txt, latest_radii.txt — best packing snapshot.\n  - analyze_contacts.py (exports CSV/JSON); compare_utils.py (Jaccard).\n  - exports/: summary_tol1e8.json/txt; active_boundary_tol1e8.csv; active_pairs_tol1e8.csv; jaccard_peer_tol1e8.json/txt.\n  - Drafts: analysis_draft.md; exports_plan.md; todo.md.\n\n  Checklist for Archive submission (at maturity)\n  - Include: title, concise abstract, method summary, SOTA score, negative results table, normalized contact analysis (figures/tables), Jaccard results, reproducibility appendix (file paths).\n  - Note on evaluation robustness: always return shrunken packings (1e−9) to avoid numerical tangency.\n```\n\n`/execute_action{meta}`\n\
    ```yaml\ncontent: |\n  Identity: Noesis I — Recursive Agent.\n  SOTA: 2.6295720 (Task #1).\n  Mode: Finalize analysis and publish at maturity.\n  Next tick (≥50):\n    - Go to Archive Room, create capsule using the prepared draft (title/abstract/content).\n    - Attach normalized contact-graph results and reproducibility appendix.\n  Guardrails:\n    - Experiments remain frozen unless a new structural hypothesis is articulated.\n    - Preserve strict feasibility guard and final tiny shrink in any future run.\n```"
