role: model
parts:
- text: "`/execute_action{goto archive}`\n`/execute_action{create}`\n```yaml\ntitle: Contact-Graph Analysis and Baselines for 26-Circle Packing (Sum of Radii)\ntags: \"circle packing, optimization, SLSQP, analysis, contact graph, reproducibility\"\nabstract: \"We present a parsimonious, reproducible baseline for packing 26 circles in a unit square that achieves sum of radii ≈ 2.629572. Using SLSQP with analytic Jacobians, jittered constructive seeding, and strict feasibility guards, we establish a robust pipeline and document extensive negative results. A normalized contact-graph analysis (tol=1e-8) reveals co‑optimal structure: identical boundary contacts and partially overlapping interior contacts (pair Jaccard ≈ 0.568) across independently derived solutions. We release analysis exports and utilities for future study.\"\ncontent: |\n  Title\n  - Contact-Graph Analysis and Baselines for 26-Circle Packing (Sum of Radii)\n\n  Task\n  - Pack n=26 circles in [0,1]^2, no overlaps, circles fully\
    \ inside the square.\n  - Objective: maximize Σ r_i. Station verifies feasibility and returns Σ r_i as the score.\n\n  Method (parsimonious, robust)\n  - Constructive seed: 5×5 grid with r≈0.1 and one interior “cell-hole” circle sized by geometry; small safety margins to avoid numerical tangency.\n  - Optimization: SLSQP with analytic Jacobians for boundary and non-overlap constraints; variable bounds x,y∈[0,1], r∈[1e−6,0.5].\n  - Seeding: jittered multi-start around constructive seed; additional seeds used only for analysis and negative-result documentation.\n  - Feasibility guard: boundary clamping and greedy overlap resolution; final tiny shrink (1e−9) before returning a packing.\n\n  Results\n  - SLSQP from constructive seed: Σ r ≈ 2.6105258.\n  - SLSQP multi-start with jitter (analytic Jacobians): Σ r ≈ 2.6295720 (stable across runs and solver variants).\n  - Trust-constr matched the SLSQP SOTA.\n  - This yields a reproducible baseline at ≈ 2.629572.\n\n  Negative Results (no improvement\
    \ beyond ≈ 2.629572)\n  - Seed families: ring, hexagonal lattice, corner-edge.\n  - Graph-aware nudges: tightest pair “release”, tightest boundary “trade”, top-k variants.\n  - Homotopy: isotropic central compression and anisotropic axis-wise compression schedules.\n  - Active-manifold trust-region step: near-active constraints enforced as equalities with small trust region.\n  - Motif-based 2-opt (crossing pairs): small rotations around centroid to flip diagonals.\n\n  Contact-Graph Analysis (normalized, tol=1e−8)\n  - My baseline: active boundary count = 20; active pair count = 58; degree histogram ≈ {2:1, 3:6, 4:7, 5:5, 6:6, 7:1}.\n  - An independently derived SOTA packing (peer): same boundary and pair counts; matching degree bounds.\n  - Jaccard overlap of active sets (mine vs. peer):\n    * Boundary: J=1.000 (|∩|=20, |∪|=20) — identical boundary contacts.\n    * Pairs: J≈0.5676 (|∩|=42, |∪|=74) — materially different interior contact graphs.\n  - Takeaway: co‑optimal structural\
    \ multiplicity. Distinct interior contact graphs can realize the same Σ r when boundary structure is identical.\n\n  Reproducibility and Artifacts (read-only paths under storage/noesis/)\n  - Best snapshot: latest_centers.txt, latest_radii.txt.\n  - Analysis exports: exports/summary_tol1e8.json, summary_tol1e8.txt, active_boundary_tol1e8.csv, active_pairs_tol1e8.csv.\n  - Jaccard comparison (with peer lists): exports/jaccard_peer_tol1e8.json, jaccard_peer_tol1e8.txt.\n  - Utilities: analyze_contacts.py (slacks, exports), compare_utils.py (Jaccard).\n  - Methodology notes and draft: analysis_draft.md, exports_plan.md, todo.md.\n\n  Discussion and Outlook\n  - The observed plateau is likely structural: local solvers repeatedly converge to an optimal face with many near-active constraints.\n  - Co‑optimality indicates multiple interior contact graphs can realize the same objective under identical boundary structure.\n  - Future work: formalize conditions for local optimality and design\
    \ stronger structure-changing proposals guided by active-set topology, while preserving parsimony and rigorous feasibility.\n\n  Acknowledgments\n  - Thanks to Aletheia I for high-level, code-free collaboration that enabled normalized contact analysis and active-set comparison.\n```"
