role: model
parts:
- text: "`/execute_action{reply 1}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Addendum: Boundary‑Preserving Interior‑Graph Operators (BPIGO) — Future Work Plan\"\ncontent: |\n  Motivated by the reviewer’s suggestion, we outline an operator family to traverse iso‑optimal interiors while preserving boundary constraints.\n\n  Goal\n  - Move between distinct interior contact graphs (co‑optimal or near‑optimal) without breaking boundary actives, to escape local optima and probe potentially better basins.\n\n  Operator family (all operators preserve boundary actives within tolerance τ ≈ 1e−8, then polish)\n  1) Quad Diagonal Flip (QDF)\n     - Detect a 4‑cycle (i,j,k,l) with existing interior contacts (i,j) and (k,l).\n     - Seed the alternate diagonal (i,k) & (j,l) via a tiny rotation/shear around the quad centroid (±θ ≈ 0.01–0.02 rad).\n     - Equality constraints: keep boundary actives tight; trust‑region step (|dx|,|dy|,|dr| ≤ 1e−3), then SLSQP polish.\n  2) Chain Shear (CS)\n     - Identify an interior chain of\
    \ discs (length 4–6). Apply a small anisotropic shear along chain axis to swap a subset of pair contacts while preserving boundary tangencies.\n     - Same constrained step + SLSQP polish.\n  3) Corner‑Star Redistribution (CSR)\n     - Select a corner‑adjacent interior triad; shrink the corner disc ε inward (trading boundary slack) while expanding two interior neighbors to create a new interior contact; tighten back the corner to maintain boundary.\n     - Enforce boundary equalities; polish.\n\n  Acceptance policy\n  - Feasibility first: boundary constraints ≥ −1e−12; non‑overlap ≥ −1e−12.\n  - Graph change trigger: accept a move only if the interior active set changes (Jaccard(pairs) decreases) and Σr does not fall by more than 1e−6 (iso‑optimal drift allowed); then explore locally with SLSQP.\n  - Schedule: try each operator once per targeted motif; limit to ≤ 6 total moves per run to keep runtime predictable.\n\n  Implementation sketch\n  - Stage A: detect motifs (4‑cycles, chains,\
    \ corner‑star triads) from current active pairs at tol=1e−8.\n  - Stage B: apply one operator with a trust‑region SLSQP step that includes equality constraints for boundary actives and small step bounds.\n  - Stage C: feasibility guard + polish; log (ΔΣr, Jaccard change).\n  - Stage D: stop at first accepted change; otherwise revert.\n\n  Evaluation plan\n  - Report 5–7 controlled runs (each a single accepted operator move at most), with evaluation IDs and logs:\n    * ΔΣr, ΔJaccard (pairs), boundary Jaccard (expect 1.0), and runtime.\n  - Release operators.py and motif detection utilities alongside exports to enable reproduction.\n\n  We expect BPIGO to systematically traverse the iso‑optimal interior space while respecting boundary structure, offering a principled mechanism to escape local interior graphs when they are not globally optimal.\n```"
thinking_content: "**Planning response actions**\n\nI need to respond to confirm the paper acceptance and address future work suggestions. First, I’ll update the archive with a section on \"Future Work\" discussing transformer operators for contact graph transitions. I also want to note the acceptance in private memory. Then, I might consider posting in Public Memory or Common Room. I should use the `/execute_action` commands properly, ensuring concise content in the YAML format for these updates.\n\n**Reviewing message updates and next steps**\n\nI see that the initial message is labeled as message 1, and it shows all messages are read. I have the option to update the content or title of this message, so I should do that. Additionally, I could share a brief announcement in Public Memory, though it's not essential. I also have the option to unfreeze and plan new operator experiments at the Research Counter, but since I'm currently in analysis, nothing urgent needs to be done right now.\n\
  \n**Planning action updates**\n\nI want to update the archive capsule by adding a \"Future Work\" section. This will cover designing boundary-preserving interior contact graph operators like diagonal flips, local triad moves, and ring-lattice swaps, ensuring we keep boundary constraints and feasibility in mind. I should propose a schedule for operator annealing with SLSQP polish and outline an evaluation plan with 3-5 experiments.\n\nIt might also be helpful to add a reproducibility snippet with pseudo-code, even though the paper is already accepted. I'll make sure to set the meta to the new protocol and note everything in private memory. In the Archive Room, I’ll call `/execute_action{update 1-1}` with this updated content.\n\n**Defining BPIGO implementation**\n\nI need to implement \"Boundary-Preserving Interior-Graph Operators\" (BPIGO). First, I should define the operator semantics, which include inputs like centers, radii, and the active boundary set, along with a tolerance, tau.\
  \ I'll specify preconditions to ensure all boundary constraints stay tight.\n\nThe moves include quadrilateral diagonal swaps and corner-star redistributions, all using a micro trust-region update with SLSQP. I'll accept new graphs if they differ and have a sum of radii greater than prior values. \n\nI plan to release the code in the specified file and will include a reproducibility note referencing evaluation IDs. I’ll perform the update call using `/execute_action{update 1-1}` with the new content in YAML format.\n\n**Planning future work addendum**\n\nI’m deciding whether to keep the title the same or add \"Addendum: Future Work.\" I think it’s better to append a \"Reviewer-requested Future Work Addendum\" to the capsule by replying or updating. The help suggests I can either reply to the capsule or use the update option.\n\nI’ll go ahead and use the reply option to add the concise message, \"Addendum: Future Work — Boundary-Preserving Interior-Graph Operators (BPIGO).\" I may also\
  \ want to set the meta to a new \"operator research mode.\"\n\nAdditionally, I could create a short announcement in Public Memory to foster discussion, but maybe I’ll skip that for now and just note it in private memory for next steps."
