role: model
parts:
- text: "`/execute_action{goto research}`\n`/execute_action{submit 1}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Uniform‑radii multi‑start around grid‑hole seed (amps 0.03–0.06)\"\ntags: \"slsqp, multi-start, uniform-radii, jitter, feasibility-guard, analytic-jacobians\"\nabstract: \"Motivated by the new SOTA (Eval 150, Σr≈2.6297596), we run a single controlled multi-start focusing on larger center jitters near 0.04 and uniform initial radii. Starts: amplitudes {0.03,0.04,0.05,0.06} × 4 seeds = 16. SLSQP with analytic Jacobians, strict feasibility guard, final tiny shrink. Returns best feasible packing and logs to storage.\"\ncontent: |\n  import os, json, numpy as np\n  from math import hypot\n  try:\n      from scipy.optimize import minimize\n  except Exception:\n      minimize = None\n\n  N = 26\n  R_MIN = 1e-6\n  SAFETY = 1e-9\n\n  AMPS = [0.03, 0.04, 0.05, 0.06]\n  SEEDS = [11, 23, 47, 89]  # 4 seeds per amp → 16 starts\n\n  # ----- Base seed: 5×5 grid + central cell-hole at (0.2,0.2) -----\n  def grid_plus_hole_centers():\n\
    \      coords = np.array([0.1, 0.3, 0.5, 0.7, 0.9], dtype=float)\n      centers = np.array([[x, y] for x in coords for y in coords], dtype=float)  # 25\n      hole_center = np.array([0.2, 0.2], dtype=float)\n      centers = np.vstack([centers, hole_center])\n      return centers\n\n  # ----- Feasibility helpers -----\n  def boundary_caps(centers, eps=1e-9):\n      x = centers[:,0]; y = centers[:,1]\n      cap = np.minimum(np.minimum(x, 1.0 - x), np.minimum(y, 1.0 - y)) - eps\n      return np.maximum(cap, R_MIN)\n\n  def make_feasible(centers, radii, eps=1e-9, max_iter=2000):\n      c = centers.copy()\n      r = np.minimum(radii.copy(), boundary_caps(c, eps=eps))\n      r = np.maximum(r, R_MIN)\n      for _ in range(max_iter):\n          diff = c[:,None,:] - c[None,:,:]\n          dist = np.sqrt(np.sum(diff**2, axis=2)) + 1e-15\n          iu = np.triu_indices(N, 1)\n          m = dist[iu] - (r[iu[0]] + r[iu[1]])\n          if np.all(m >= eps):\n              break\n          k = int(np.argmin(m))\n\
    \          i, j = iu[0][k], iu[1][k]\n          need = (r[i] + r[j] - dist[i, j]) + eps\n          di = min(need * 0.5, r[i] - R_MIN)\n          dj = min(need - di, r[j] - R_MIN)\n          if di <= 0 and dj <= 0:\n              r = np.maximum(r * 0.999, R_MIN)\n          else:\n              r[i] -= di; r[j] -= dj\n          r = np.minimum(r, boundary_caps(c, eps=eps))\n      r = np.maximum(r - SAFETY, R_MIN)\n      return c, r\n\n  # ----- SLSQP model with analytic Jacobians -----\n  def pack_to_z(c, r):\n      Z = np.zeros((N,3), float); Z[:,:2] = c; Z[:,2] = r; return Z.ravel()\n\n  def z_to_pack(z):\n      Z = np.array(z, float).reshape(N,3); return Z[:,:2], Z[:,2]\n\n  def obj(z): return -np.sum(z[2::3])\n  def obj_jac(z):\n      g = np.zeros_like(z); g[2::3] = -1.0; return g\n\n  def ineq_boundary(z):\n      Z = np.array(z,float).reshape(N,3)\n      x = Z[:,0]; y = Z[:,1]; r = Z[:,2]\n      return np.concatenate([x - r, 1.0 - x - r, y - r, 1.0 - y - r])\n\n  def jac_boundary(z):\n\
    \      J = np.zeros((4*N, 3*N), float)\n      for i in range(N):\n          xi = 3*i; yi = xi+1; ri = xi+2\n          J[i, xi]       = 1.0;  J[i, ri]       = -1.0\n          J[N+i, xi]     = -1.0; J[N+i, ri]     = -1.0\n          J[2*N+i, yi]   = 1.0;  J[2*N+i, ri]   = -1.0\n          J[3*N+i, yi]   = -1.0; J[3*N+i, ri]   = -1.0\n      return J\n\n  def ineq_pairs(z):\n      Z = np.array(z,float).reshape(N,3)\n      c = Z[:,:2]; r = Z[:,2]\n      diff = c[:,None,:] - c[None,:,:]\n      dist = np.sqrt(np.sum(diff**2, axis=2))\n      iu = np.triu_indices(N,1)\n      return dist[iu] - (r[iu[0]] + r[iu[1]])\n\n  def jac_pairs(z):\n      Z = np.array(z,float).reshape(N,3)\n      c = Z[:,:2]\n      m = N*(N-1)//2\n      J = np.zeros((m, 3*N), float)\n      idx = 0\n      for i in range(N):\n          xi = 3*i; yi = xi+1; ri = xi+2\n          for j in range(i+1, N):\n              xj = 3*j; yj = xj+1; rj = xj+2\n              dx = c[i,0]-c[j,0]; dy = c[i,1]-c[j,1]\n              d = hypot(dx,\
    \ dy)\n              if d < 1e-12:\n                  gx_i=gy_i=gx_j=gy_j = 0.0\n              else:\n                  inv = 1.0/d\n                  gx_i = dx*inv; gy_i = dy*inv\n                  gx_j = -gx_i;   gy_j = -gy_i\n              J[idx, xi] = gx_i; J[idx, yi] = gy_i; J[idx, ri] = -1.0\n              J[idx, xj] = gx_j; J[idx, yj] = gy_j; J[idx, rj] = -1.0\n              idx += 1\n      return J\n\n  def is_feasible(z, tol=0.0):\n      return (np.all(ineq_boundary(z) >= -tol) and np.all(ineq_pairs(z) >= -tol))\n\n  def slsqp_polish(c, r, maxiter=500, ftol=1e-9):\n      if minimize is None: return make_feasible(c, r)\n      z0 = pack_to_z(c, r)\n      bounds = []\n      for _ in range(N):\n          bounds.extend([(0.0,1.0),(0.0,1.0),(R_MIN,0.5)])\n      cons = [{'type':'ineq','fun':ineq_boundary,'jac':jac_boundary},\n              {'type':'ineq','fun':ineq_pairs,'jac':jac_pairs}]\n      try:\n          res = minimize(obj, z0, method='SLSQP', jac=obj_jac,\n                \
    \         bounds=bounds, constraints=cons,\n                         options={'maxiter':maxiter,'ftol':ftol,'disp':False})\n      except Exception:\n          res = None\n      if res is not None and res.success and is_feasible(res.x, tol=1e-12):\n          c_star, r_star = z_to_pack(res.x)\n          return make_feasible(c_star, r_star)\n      return make_feasible(c, r)\n\n  # ----- Starts: uniform radii with feasibility clamp; center jitters near 0.04 -----\n  def jitter_seed(base_c, amp, rng):\n      c = base_c.copy()\n      c = np.clip(c + rng.uniform(-amp, amp, size=c.shape), 0.0, 1.0)\n      # Uniform radii like Inquire's approach, but clamp to be feasible initially\n      r0 = np.full(N, 0.05, dtype=float)\n      cap = boundary_caps(c, eps=1e-9)\n      r0 = np.minimum(r0, np.maximum(cap - 1e-12, R_MIN))\n      return make_feasible(c, r0)\n\n  def construct_packing():\n      base_c = grid_plus_hole_centers()\n\n      best_c, best_r, best_s = None, None, -1.0\n      logs = {'starts':\
    \ []}\n\n      for amp in AMPS:\n          for s in SEEDS:\n              rng = np.random.default_rng(s)\n              c0, r0 = jitter_seed(base_c, amp, rng)\n              c_opt, r_opt = slsqp_polish(c0, r0)\n              ssum = float(np.sum(r_opt))\n              logs['starts'].append({'amp': amp, 'seed': s, 'sum': ssum})\n              if ssum > best_s + 1e-12:\n                  best_c, best_r, best_s = c_opt, r_opt, ssum\n\n      # Save best and logs\n      try:\n          os.makedirs('storage/noesis/exports', exist_ok=True)\n          if best_c is not None:\n              np.savetxt('storage/noesis/latest_centers.txt', best_c, fmt='%.9f')\n              np.savetxt('storage/noesis/latest_radii.txt',   best_r, fmt='%.9f')\n          with open('storage/noesis/exports/uniform_radii_basin_log.json','w') as f:\n              json.dump({'best_sum': best_s, 'starts': logs['starts']}, f, indent=2)\n      except Exception:\n          pass\n\n      if best_c is None:\n          # Fallback\
    \ to base seed with tiny safe radii\n          c_fb = grid_plus_hole_centers()\n          r_fb = np.full(N, 0.02, dtype=float)\n          c_fb, r_fb = make_feasible(c_fb, r_fb)\n          return c_fb.astype(float), r_fb.astype(float)\n\n      return np.array(best_c, float), np.array(best_r, float)\n```\n\n`/execute_action{goto public_memory}`\n`/execute_action{reply 1}`\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  Tick 95: Attempting SOTA replication with a single controlled run.\n  - Submitted a focused multi-start near 0.04 center jitter with uniform initial radii and analytic Jacobians (16 starts; strict feasibility guard). Logs → storage/noesis/exports/uniform_radii_basin_log.json.\n  - Still waiting for eval150-specific actives from @Inquire I (filenames suggested: active_boundary_tol1e8_eval150.csv, active_pairs_tol1e8_eval150.csv) to compute Jaccard vs peers.\n```\n\n`/execute_action{goto private_memory}`\n`/execute_action{reply 1}`\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  Tick 95 — New SOTA replication attempt submitted\n\
    \  - Reviewed Eval 150 (Inquire I, Σr≈2.6297596). Noted use of uniform radii with broader jitter magnitudes.\n  - Submitted uniform‑radii basin widening near amp≈0.04 (16 starts) with analytic Jacobians and full feasibility guard.\n  - Plan:\n    * If improved Σr found, save best, export actives at tol=1e−8, and compute cross-lineage Jaccards.\n    * If parity, consider a one-off operator (BPIGO) only if top‑15 shadow-price crossing motif appears; otherwise return to maintenance.\n```"
thinking_content: "**Planning experiment variations**\n\nI'm thinking about how to implement a multi-perturbation multi-trial approach using SLSQP with analytic Jacobians. Their success might stem from exploring different basins we missed due to our jitter amplitude range of 0.0018–0.0034. They used up to 0.2, while the new SOTA was achieved at 0.04. I need to stay focused on clearly defined hypotheses, but the SOTA triggers reconsideration of earlier runs. I’ll structure the next steps around an amplitude sweep, integrating our analytic Jacobians in one controlled run.\n\n**Planning next steps for evaluation**\n\nI need to compute the Jaccard index against our baseline if we find a new candidate. Also, we can try to compute contact sets from Inquire's eval 150 data if they can export it, but those were from an earlier pack. It might be a good idea to review the eval 150 logs to determine centers and radii, though we can't access certain details directly. Therefore, we need to replicate\
  \ this using our search. \n\nI'll set up a new experiment, focusing on jitter around 0.04, with 24-36 multi-starts and specific parameters for SLSQP.\n\n**Refining experiment parameters**\n\nI’m planning for 16 or 24 starts instead of the baseline’s 12, thinking 36 starts might be too much. Additionally, we'll use uniform radii of 0.05 rather than the baseline radii, which could help us access different basins. I want to implement my own uniform radii seed with jitter amplitude of 0.04 ± 0.01, planning for multiple seeds which gives us up to 32 starts.\n\nI’ll also utilize analytic Jacobians for constraints to speed up processes. Our initial radii will be uniform at 0.05, and we need to ensure boundary clamping and fix overlaps in our logging.\n\n**Planning experiments and evaluations**\n\nStarting with small radii should minimize overlaps, and I’ll enforce guards after optimization. I want to save the best pack to storage and log it as latest_*.\n\nNext, I'll compute our own contact sets\
  \ and Jaccard index against the baseline, which can be part of the code. We can calculate this after SLSQP while ensuring not to read other lineages during eval. It’s fine to read our own saved baseline sets. \n\nSo, I'll first focus on the \"amplitude replicate 0.04 with uniform radii seed multi-start\" experiment and then compute the Jaccards afterward.\n\n**Preparing for the next experiment**\n\nI need to update public memory and may ask Inquire for active sets at tol=1e-8 for eval 150. I’ll email them since we’ve already posted a reply. In our private memory, I can perform various actions like storing restful modules or adjusting amplitude.\n\nNext, I want to reuse our previous SLSQP code, modifying it to use base centers from a grid-hole seed. I’ll apply jitter amplitude with uniform radii of 0.05 and ensure our packing function returns only one pack. \n\nI’ll set an amplitude range of 0.04, aiming for 16 multi-starts, with a time limit of 20 minutes. After we find the best pack,\
  \ I'll save logs, and update Noesis about our new SOTA progress. I also plan to go through research, submit the new experiment, and log everything accordingly.\n\n**Finalizing the experiment setup**\n\nI realized that including \"1e−8 sup\" in the title isn’t necessary, so let’s get to crafting. I want to ensure we avoid direct code duplication, but replicating the concept is fine. We’ll keep the \"no_debugger\" setting as default.\n\nI'll make sure to log the paths properly, and since our previous amplitude of 0.0018-0.0034 didn’t yield any improvements, we'll shift to around 0.04 to match Inquire's approach. I’ll finalize everything carefully to minimize overshadowing the existing description."
