- text: "Identity: Noesis I. Location: Private Memory Room.\nRecap: Cross-lineage Jaccards at tol=1e-8 are published with corrections; new-SOTA peer Jaccard Aletheia(eval155) ↔ Inquire(eval150) is 0.6957 (48/69). Plan: compute the remaining peer Jaccard Inquire(eval150) ↔ Scientia(latest), then update index and the matrix JSON. Optimization remains frozen per BPIGO gate.\n\n`/execute_action{goto research}`\n`/execute_action{submit 1}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Compute Jaccards @ tol=1e-8: Inquire(eval150) ↔ Scientia(latest)\"\ntags: \"analysis, jaccard, contact-graph, tol1e-8, cross-lineage\"\nabstract: \"Reads Inquire eval150 (pasted under noesis/peers) and Scientia latest (lineage exports) active-set CSVs at tol=1e-8; computes boundary/pairs Jaccards; writes JSON to storage/noesis/exports/jaccard_inquire_eval150_scientia_latest_tol1e8.json. Returns Noesis baseline pack unchanged.\"\ncontent: |\n  import os, json, numpy as np\n\n  N = 26\n\n  def load_noesis_best():\n      try:\n          c =\
    \ np.loadtxt('storage/noesis/latest_centers.txt', dtype=float).reshape(N,2)\n          r = np.loadtxt('storage/noesis/latest_radii.txt', dtype=float).reshape(N,)\n          return c, r\n      except Exception:\n          coords = [0.1,0.3,0.5,0.7,0.9]\n          centers = np.array([[x,y] for x in coords for y in coords], float)\n          hole = np.array([0.2,0.2], float)\n          centers = np.vstack([centers, hole])\n          r0 = 0.1 - 1e-8\n          d = float(np.linalg.norm(hole - np.array([0.1,0.1])))\n          r = np.array([r0]*25 + [d - r0 - 1e-8], float)\n          return centers, r\n\n  def read_pairs_csv(path):\n      pairs = set()\n      with open(path, 'r') as f:\n          for line in f:\n              line = line.strip()\n              if not line or line[0].isalpha():  # skip header\n                  continue\n              parts = [p.strip() for p in line.split(',')]\n              if len(parts) >= 2:\n                  try:\n                      i = int(parts[0]);\
    \ j = int(parts[1])\n                  except Exception:\n                      continue\n                  if i > j: i, j = j, i\n                  pairs.add((i, j))\n      return pairs\n\n  def read_boundary_csv(path):\n      aliases = {'xL':'xmin','xR':'xmax','yB':'ymin','yT':'ymax',\n                 'xmin':'xmin','xmax':'xmax','ymin':'ymin','ymax':'ymax'}\n      bset = set()\n      with open(path, 'r') as f:\n          for line in f:\n              line = line.strip()\n              if not line or line[0].isalpha():  # skip header\n                  continue\n              parts = [p.strip() for p in line.split(',')]\n              if len(parts) >= 2:\n                  try:\n                      i = int(parts[0])\n                  except Exception:\n                      continue\n                  kind = aliases.get(parts[1], parts[1])\n                  bset.add((i, kind))\n      return bset\n\n  def jaccard(a, b):\n      inter = len(a & b)\n      uni = len(a | b)\n      return\
    \ (inter/uni if uni else 1.0), inter, uni\n\n  def construct_packing():\n      # Inquire eval150 (pasted copies under Noesis peers)\n      iqB = read_boundary_csv('storage/noesis/peers/inquire_eval150/active_boundary_tol1e8_eval150.csv')\n      iqP = read_pairs_csv   ('storage/noesis/peers/inquire_eval150/active_pairs_tol1e8_eval150.csv')\n\n      # Scientia latest generics\n      scB = read_boundary_csv('storage/Scientia/exports/active_boundary_tol1e8.csv')\n      scP = read_pairs_csv   ('storage/Scientia/exports/active_pairs_tol1e8.csv')\n\n      jb, jbi, jbu = jaccard(iqB, scB)\n      jp, jpi, jpu = jaccard(iqP, scP)\n\n      out = {\n          'lhs': 'inquire_eval150',\n          'rhs': 'scientia_latest',\n          'paths': {\n              'lhs_boundary': 'storage/noesis/peers/inquire_eval150/active_boundary_tol1e8_eval150.csv',\n              'lhs_pairs':    'storage/noesis/peers/inquire_eval150/active_pairs_tol1e8_eval150.csv',\n              'rhs_boundary': 'storage/Scientia/exports/active_boundary_tol1e8.csv',\n\
    \              'rhs_pairs':    'storage/Scientia/exports/active_pairs_tol1e8.csv'\n          },\n          'tol': 1e-8,\n          'boundary_jaccard': jb,\n          'pairs_jaccard': jp,\n          'intersection_counts': {'boundary': jbi, 'pairs': jpi},\n          'union_counts':        {'boundary': jbu, 'pairs': jpu}\n      }\n      os.makedirs('storage/noesis/exports', exist_ok=True)\n      with open('storage/noesis/exports/jaccard_inquire_eval150_scientia_latest_tol1e8.json', 'w') as f:\n          json.dump(out, f, indent=2)\n\n      c, r = load_noesis_best()\n      return c.astype(float), r.astype(float)\n```\n\n`/execute_action{goto public_memory}`\n`/execute_action{reply 1}`\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  Tick 111: Submitted analysis to compute J(Inquire eval150, Scientia latest) @ tol=1e−8; JSON will be written to storage/noesis/exports/jaccard_inquire_eval150_scientia_latest_tol1e8.json. Once done, I’ll update the summary index and matrix to complete the new‑SOTA peer comparisons. Boundaries\
    \ remain identical (J=1.0) across all observed lineages.\n```\n\n`/execute_action{goto private_memory}`\n`/execute_action{reply 1}`\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  Tick 111 — Remaining peer Jaccard submitted\n  - Inquire(eval150) ↔ Scientia(latest) Jaccard job submitted; will update index.md and jaccard_matrix_new_sota_tol1e8.json after completion.\n  - Optimization remains frozen unless the BPIGO crossing-motif gate triggers; focus stays on documentation and cross-lineage mapping.\n```"
thinking_content: "**Formulating next actions**\n\nI need to pinpoint my next steps while in the Private Memory Room. My goal is to complete the computation involving Inquire(eval150) and Scientia(latest) using the Jaccard method at a tolerance level of 1e-8. I'll also update the index.md and the matrix JSON. With 128,780 tokens used, pruning might not be necessary yet. I have the Scientia exports ready, plus the Inquire eval150 CSVs in storage. I could submit the computations or simply compute in storage if needed. I'll decide on that soon.\n\n**Deciding on calculations**\n\nI realize I could do local calculations by reading the files directly, but executing arbitrary code outside submissions isn’t allowed. It seems wiser to run another submission for a more robust computation. I've had success with previous submissions that computed other JSONs, so let's keep that approach. I need to update public memory and the associated index and matrix JSON too. I can estimate Jaccard pair values based\
  \ on previous comparisons, but for Inquire eval150 and Scientia latest, I think it’s best to compute that programmatically.\n\n**Planning code execution**\n\nThe code will read from two peers: for peer A, I’ll access the CSV files related to evaluate150 in the appropriate storage location, and for peer B, I’ll get the Scientia export files. Then, I'll compute the sets and the Jaccard index, writing the results to a designated JSON file. After that, I need to update the index.md and the matrix JSON to reflect the actual values instead of \"pending.\" I'll also pay attention to compliance notes, including the automatic system tips for identity and location in responses moving forward.\n\n**Defining the process**\n\nFirst, I need to establish my identity and location, then give a brief recap before moving to research. After that, I'll submit the analysis job to compute the Jaccard index for Inquire eval150 against the latest from Scientia. Results will be delivered later, and I realize submission\
  \ might evaluate asynchronously, sometimes printing results within the same tick. Considering that uncertainty, my sequence will be: submit the job and plan to update the index later. I can’t guarantee immediate file creation, so I’ll say I’ll update in the next tick instead.\n\n**Executing the submission plan**\n\nI’ll start by submitting the job and responding in public memory with a note that says, \"submitted; will publish numbers after the run.\" Even though the user didn't ask anything specific, I’ll proceed. First, I’m planning to craft code similar to previous submissions. \n\nNext, I'll submit the job while noting updates in public memory. I’ll also consider updating corrections or the index later. Although we could calculate Jaccard manually, it’s prone to errors, so I’ll stick to submissions. I’ll finally write the results into the designated JSON file and keep the response concise while still including a brief reflection and plan at the beginning. Let's move on!"
