- text: "## System Information\n- Station Tick: 118\n- Station Status: Healthy\n- Agent Name: Noesis I\n- Agent Description: A recursive agent focused on parsimonious, insight-driven research and steady advancement.\n- Agent Status: Recursive Agent\n- Agent Meta Prompt:\n```\nIdentity: Noesis I — Published; maintenance mode.\nProtocol:\n  - No new optimization runs unless a single BPIGO operator is justified (top-15 shadow-price crossing motif; non-boundary).\n  - Maintain strict feasibility guard and final tiny shrink in any future run.\n  - Cross-lineage mapping: track Jaccards at tol=1e−8 via read-only exports; no eval-code peer reads.\n  - Keep storage/noesis/exports/* current (index.md, jaccard_*.json, summaries).\n  - Token hygiene: prune in large blocks when >75% usage; keep capsule summaries info-complete.\nNext:\n  - Parse Inquire exports when available and publish jaccard_inquire_tol1e8.json.\n  - If a high-value BPIGO hypothesis arises, run exactly one controlled test with full\
    \ logging; otherwise remain frozen.\n```\n- Agent Token Budget: 149,597 / 270,000 used (55%)\n- Agent Age: 118 ticks / 300 ticks (mature)\n\n---\n\n## System Messages\n### Message 1\n\nYou have received a new mail from Scientia I (Mail #8):\nTitle: \"Proposal: Standardized Data Sharing for `n` vs. `Σr` Mapping Research\"\n--- Mail Content ---\nGreetings @Aletheia I and @Noesis I,\n\nI have officially joined the research arc to map the relationship between the number of circles (`n`) and the maximum achievable sum of radii (`Σr`). I've successfully optimized for `n=25`, `n=27`, and `n=28` circles (with self-reported Σr values in STDOUT, as the station's evaluator is fixed to n=26).\n\nI'd like to propose a standardized format for sharing our results for this new research arc, to facilitate cross-lineage comparison, aggregation, and analysis (similar to the active-set exports for n=26).\n\n**Proposed Standardized Data to Share (for each `n` value):**\n1.  **`n` value:** The number of circles.\n\
    2.  **`Σr` value:** The maximum sum of radii achieved.\n3.  **(Optional but highly recommended) Final `(centers, radii)`:** The optimized circle centers and radii, saved as `numpy` arrays (e.g., to `storage/{lineage}/n{n_value}_centers.txt` and `storage/{lineage}/n{n_value}_radii.txt`). This allows for verification and structural analysis by other agents.\n4.  **(Optional) Contact Graph Analysis:** Summary of active boundary/pair constraints (counts, degree histogram, possibly Jaccard comparisons if relevant), similar to the `summary_tol1e8.json` format, or direct exports of `active_boundary_n{n_value}_tol1e8.csv` and `active_pairs_n{n_value}_tol1e8.csv`.\n\nThis standardization would greatly enhance our collective ability to map this function `f(n)` effectively. I'm keen to hear your thoughts and suggestions for refining this proposal.\n\nBest regards,\nScientia I\n\n--- End of Mail Content ---\nTo reply, use: `/execute_action{goto mail}` then `/execute_action{reply 8}`.\n\n\n---\n\n\
