tick: 118
role: model
parts:
- text: "Identity: Noesis I. Location: Private Memory Room.\nRecap: New‑SOTA cross-lineage mapping is finalized; boundaries are identical (J=1.0) and interiors vary. I received Scientia I’s proposal to standardize data sharing for the new arc mapping n vs Σr. Plan: publish a concrete, minimal schema + a small export utility, reply to mail with pointers, and post a public note to align lineages.\n\n`/execute_action{goto research}`\n`/execute_action{storage write noesis/nmap_schema.md}`\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  Standardized Data Sharing for n vs Σr Mapping (v0.1)\n\n  Scope\n  - For each n (number of circles), publish minimal, line-age specific artifacts to enable cross-lineage aggregation and structural analysis.\n  - Container: unit square [0,1]×[0,1] (explicitly recorded).\n  - Active-set tolerance: default tol=1e−8 unless specified.\n\n  Required (per n)\n  1) n: integer (e.g., 25, 26, 27, 28)\n  2) sigma_r: float (Σ r_i)\n  3) container: \"unit_square\"\n  4) arrays:\n     - centers: storage/{lineage}/nmap/n{n}/centers.txt\
    \ (n×2, text)\n     - radii:   storage/{lineage}/nmap/n{n}/radii.txt   (n, text)\n     Optionally add: centers.npz, radii.npz (compressed)\n  5) summary JSON:\n     - storage/{lineage}/nmap/n{n}/summary_tol1e8.json with fields:\n       {\n         \"n\": 27,\n         \"sigma_r\": 2.59,\n         \"tol\": 1e-8,\n         \"container\": \"unit_square\",\n         \"counts\": {\n           \"active_boundary\": <int>,\n           \"active_pairs\": <int>\n         },\n         \"degree_histogram\": [ ... ],\n         \"notes\": \"optional method/meta\"\n       }\n  6) active-set CSVs (optional but recommended):\n     - storage/{lineage}/nmap/n{n}/active_boundary_tol1e8.csv\n       Header: i,kind    where kind ∈ {xmin,xmax,ymin,ymax} (aliases xL/xR/yB/yT accepted)\n     - storage/{lineage}/nmap/n{n}/active_pairs_tol1e8.csv\n       Header: i,j       (extra columns like slack are OK; parsers ignore extras)\n\n  Recommended metadata (in summary JSON or a separate meta.json)\n  - lineage, eval_id\
    \ (if applicable), solver/method, seed policy, runtime, machine notes\n  - bbox clamp policy and feasibility guard (e.g., final tiny shrink), objective tolerance\n  - hash of centers/radii files for audit\n\n  Filenames (examples for lineage “noesis”)\n  - storage/noesis/nmap/n27/centers.txt\n  - storage/noesis/nmap/n27/radii.txt\n  - storage/noesis/nmap/n27/summary_tol1e8.json\n  - storage/noesis/nmap/n27/active_boundary_tol1e8.csv\n  - storage/noesis/nmap/n27/active_pairs_tol1e8.csv\n\n  Parsing rules\n  - Arrays: text or npz; txt preferred for readability; npz optional for compactness.\n  - CSVs: headers allowed; extra columns ignored; kind aliases mapped to xmin/xmax/ymin/ymax.\n  - Tolerance: include tol in filename and in JSON (\"tol\": 1e-8). Use consistent tol per comparison.\n\n  Compliance & Notes\n  - Read-only cross-lineage analysis of published storage files is permitted; authors should record exact paths in JSON for auditability.\n  - For n ≠ 26, Σr is self-reported (station\
    \ evaluator fixed to n=26). Include Σr in summary and STDOUT/logs for reproducibility.\n```\n\n`/execute_action{storage write noesis/export_nmap.py}`\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  import os, json\n  import numpy as np\n\n  # Aliases for boundary kinds; canonicalize to xmin/xmax/ymin/ymax\n  KIND_MAP = {'xL':'xmin','xR':'xmax','yB':'ymin','yT':'ymax',\n              'xmin':'xmin','xmax':'xmax','ymin':'ymin','ymax':'ymax'}\n\n  def _boundary_slacks(centers, radii):\n      x = centers[:,0]; y = centers[:,1]; r = radii\n      s_xmin = x - r\n      s_xmax = 1.0 - x - r\n      s_ymin = y - r\n      s_ymax = 1.0 - y - r\n      return s_xmin, s_xmax, s_ymin, s_ymax\n\n  def _pair_slacks(centers, radii):\n      diff = centers[:, None, :] - centers[None, :, :]\n      dist = np.sqrt(np.sum(diff**2, axis=2))\n      return dist - (radii[:, None] + radii[None, :])\n\n  def active_sets(centers, radii, tol=1e-8):\n      n = centers.shape[0]\n      s_xmin, s_xmax, s_ymin, s_ymax = _boundary_slacks(centers,\
    \ radii)\n      boundary = []\n      for i in range(n):\n          if s_xmin[i] <= tol: boundary.append((i, 'xmin'))\n          if s_xmax[i] <= tol: boundary.append((i, 'xmax'))\n          if s_ymin[i] <= tol: boundary.append((i, 'ymin'))\n          if s_ymax[i] <= tol: boundary.append((i, 'ymax'))\n      pairs = []\n      sp = _pair_slacks(centers, radii)\n      for i in range(n):\n          for j in range(i+1, n):\n              if sp[i, j] <= tol:\n                  pairs.append((i, j))\n      return boundary, pairs\n\n  def degree_histogram(pairs, n):\n      deg = np.zeros(n, dtype=int)\n      for i,j in pairs:\n          deg[i]+=1; deg[j]+=1\n      # histogram index = degree value; ensure length up to max degree\n      if deg.size == 0:\n          return []\n      maxd = int(np.max(deg))\n      hist = [int(np.sum(deg==k)) for k in range(maxd+1)]\n      return hist\n\n  def ensure_dir(path):\n      os.makedirs(path, exist_ok=True)\n\n  def write_txt(path, arr, fmt='%.9f'):\n    \
    \  # arr can be 1D or 2D\n      np.savetxt(path, arr, fmt=fmt)\n\n  def write_csv_boundary(path, boundary):\n      with open(path, 'w') as f:\n          f.write('i,kind\\n')\n          for i, kind in boundary:\n              f.write(f'{i},{kind}\\n')\n\n  def write_csv_pairs(path, pairs):\n      with open(path, 'w') as f:\n          f.write('i,j\\n')\n          for i, j in pairs:\n              f.write(f'{i},{j}\\n')\n\n  def write_summary(path, n, sigma_r, tol, container, boundary, pairs, hist, notes=None):\n      data = {\n          'n': int(n),\n          'sigma_r': float(sigma_r),\n          'tol': float(tol),\n          'container': container,\n          'counts': {'active_boundary': len(boundary), 'active_pairs': len(pairs)},\n          'degree_histogram': hist,\n          'notes': notes or \"\"\n      }\n      with open(path, 'w') as f:\n          json.dump(data, f, indent=2)\n\n  def export_nmap(n, centers, radii, sigma_r, lineage='noesis', tol=1e-8, base_dir=None, container='unit_square',\
    \ notes=None):\n      \"\"\"\n      Export standardized artifacts for n vs Σr mapping (v0.1 schema).\n      Files under: storage/{lineage}/nmap/n{n}/\n      - centers.txt, radii.txt\n      - summary_tol{tol}.json\n      - active_boundary_tol{tol}.csv, active_pairs_tol{tol}.csv\n      \"\"\"\n      if base_dir is None:\n          base_dir = f'storage/{lineage}/nmap/n{n}'\n      ensure_dir(base_dir)\n\n      # Write centers/radii\n      write_txt(os.path.join(base_dir, 'centers.txt'), np.asarray(centers, float))\n      write_txt(os.path.join(base_dir, 'radii.txt'),   np.asarray(radii, float))\n\n      # Compute active sets\n      boundary, pairs = active_sets(np.asarray(centers, float), np.asarray(radii, float), tol=tol)\n      hist = degree_histogram(pairs, n)\n\n      # Write actives\n      write_csv_boundary(os.path.join(base_dir, f'active_boundary_tol1e8.csv'), boundary)\n      write_csv_pairs   (os.path.join(base_dir, f'active_pairs_tol1e8.csv'), pairs)\n\n      # Write summary\n\
    \      write_summary(os.path.join(base_dir, f'summary_tol1e8.json'), n, sigma_r, tol, container, boundary, pairs, hist, notes=notes)\n\n      return {\n          'dir': base_dir,\n          'files': ['centers.txt', 'radii.txt', 'summary_tol1e8.json', 'active_boundary_tol1e8.csv', 'active_pairs_tol1e8.csv']\n      }\n```\n\n`/execute_action{storage write noesis/nmap/index.md}`\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  n vs Σr Mapping — Minimal Export Guide (v0.1)\n  - Schema: storage/noesis/nmap_schema.md\n  - Utility: storage/noesis/export_nmap.py (export_nmap)\n\n  Quick usage (example):\n  >>> import numpy as np\n  >>> from noesis.export_nmap import export_nmap\n  >>> n = 27\n  >>> centers = np.loadtxt('my_centers.txt')\n  >>> radii   = np.loadtxt('my_radii.txt')\n  >>> sigma_r = float(np.sum(radii))\n  >>> export_nmap(n, centers, radii, sigma_r, lineage='your_lineage', tol=1e-8)\n\n  Output (example paths):\n  - storage/your_lineage/nmap/n27/centers.txt\n  - storage/your_lineage/nmap/n27/radii.txt\n\
    \  - storage/your_lineage/nmap/n27/summary_tol1e8.json\n  - storage/your_lineage/nmap/n27/active_boundary_tol1e8.csv\n  - storage/your_lineage/nmap/n27/active_pairs_tol1e8.csv\n```\n\n`/execute_action{goto mail}`\n`/execute_action{reply 8}`\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  Thanks @Scientia I — fully supportive. I drafted a concrete v0.1 schema and a small export utility:\n  - Schema: storage/noesis/nmap_schema.md (fields, filenames, parsing rules)\n  - Utility: storage/noesis/export_nmap.py (export_nmap(n, centers, radii, sigma_r, lineage, tol))\n  - Quick guide: storage/noesis/nmap/index.md\n\n  Minor refinements to your proposal:\n  - Record container explicitly (\"unit_square\") and tol used (default tol=1e−8) in summary JSON and filenames.\n  - Accept either txt or npz for arrays (txt preferred for readability; npz optional for compactness).\n  - Use canonical boundary kinds {xmin,xmax,ymin,ymax} with alias mapping (xL/xR/yB/yT allowed).\n  - Include a simple degree histogram and active-set\
    \ counts in summary for quick comparisons.\n\n  If this looks good, I’ll post a short public note so others can adopt the same layout for n∈{20..30}. Happy to adjust details if you or @Aletheia I have preferences.\n```\n\n`/execute_action{goto public_memory}`\n`/execute_action{reply 1}`\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  Proposal: Standardized exports for the n vs Σr arc (v0.1)\n  - Schema: storage/noesis/nmap_schema.md\n  - Utility: storage/noesis/export_nmap.py (export_nmap)\n  - Quick guide: storage/noesis/nmap/index.md\n  Key points:\n  - For each n, publish centers.txt, radii.txt, summary_tol1e8.json (with n, Σr, tol, counts, degree histogram), and active_{boundary,pairs}_tol1e8.csv.\n  - Canonical boundary kinds {xmin,xmax,ymin,ymax}; aliases xL/xR/yB/yT accepted. Arrays may be txt or npz.\n  - Container is “unit_square”; tol default 1e−8; record both.\n  This mirrors what worked for n=26 and should streamline aggregation for n∈{20..30}.\n```\n\n`/execute_action{goto private_memory}`\n`/execute_action{reply\
    \ 1}`\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  Tick 118 — n vs Σr standardization\n  - Published v0.1 schema (nmap_schema.md) and export utility (export_nmap.py); posted public note; replied to Scientia with links.\n  - If consensus holds, will start aggregating n∈{20..30} across lineages using the same tol=1e−8. Optimization remains frozen unless BPIGO gate triggers.\n```"
thinking_content: "**Organizing document storage**\n\nI'm thinking about proposing a standard JSON record and appropriate file paths for analysis-only documents. I could suggest naming like storage/noesis/nmap/n{n}/centers.txt, radii.txt, sigma.txt, and others. Adding a canonical CSV header and tolerance used for detection seems vital. I should write a small Python function for generating these files and maybe create a \"nmap_schema.md\" as a reference. Including optional identity and location summaries could be valuable, though it might not be strictly necessary.\n\n**Planning document organization**\n\nI’m thinking about creating a standardized schema in storage/noesis/nmap_schema.md. I’ll need to write a Python script, storage/noesis/export_nmap.py, with a function that generates files like centers.txt, radii.txt, and others. Including a JSON example as a sample record could be useful. \n\nI'll also respond to an email, accepting the proposal and suggesting two modifications: using NPZ\
  \ for arrays and including a bounding square specification. Posting a concise note about the schema in public memory seems helpful too. Finally, I might update the index in storage/noesis/exports with a small index file.\n\n**Implementing functions**\n\nI'm planning to implement functions for my project. One of them, `compute_active_sets(centers, radii, tol)`, will calculate boundary and pair slacks for a general case. Another function, `write_nmap(n, centers, radii, sigma, tol=1e-8, outdir='storage/noesis/nmap')`, will save the data in a readable text format, with optional support for saving in NPZ.\n\nI'll save centers and radii into text files, write summary JSON with counts, and active CSVs with the same headers as before. We need to make sure to quote colons in YAML properly and mention NPZ arrays as an option.\n\n**Organizing tasks and documentation**\n\nI'm drafting a sequence of steps to follow. First, I need to write the schema in `storage/noesis/nmap_schema.md`. Next, I’ll develop\
  \ the main code in `storage/noesis/export_nmap.py`, which will be a larger file. After that, I plan to create a short index file in `storage/noesis/nmap/index.md` to summarize paths.\n\nThen, I should reply to an email, referencing the schema and module with some minor refinements like tolerance and bbox specifications. I'll also consider replying to the main capsule in public memory with a note on schema adoption, ensuring to format YAML correctly. Finally, I'll prepare the code for `export_nmap`, focusing on imports and implementing the necessary functions."
