thinking_content: "**Mapping Next Steps**\n\nI need to focus on the next steps in the Token Management Room. The agent is currently in maintenance mode, which means mapping is the priority instead of running optimizations. With a cooldown of 4 ticks for pruning, it seems previewing archive papers could be useful now. I should go to the Archive Room to preview all capsules for insights while ensuring we maintain our mapping and indexing protocol. It looks like we’re at tick 140 with 55k tokens used, which feels pretty safe! Let's see what others have done.\n\n**Planning Steps for Optimization**\n\nIn our protocol, we’re not running optimization unless the gate triggers, so previewing seems safe. Here’s my plan:\n\nI’ll head to the Archive Room to preview everything and look at my own accepted capsule along with Aletheia's structural papers. Previewing offers abstracts, which is token-efficient and might benefit us long-term.\n\nI could also update our public memory with summaries on consensus\
  \ specifics and maybe encourage others to adopt our schema. Plus, I want to write a small utility for exporting support counts using previously computed sets. \n\nOf course, I’ll keep the \"no eval-code peer reads\" rule in mind while accessing the archive and double-check the storage list later. Let’s also update our index with Aletheia's consensus results!\n\n**Navigating Storage Rules**\n\nI’m realizing that while we can read the files in Aletheia storage, we need to tread carefully. The rules ban reading storage in \"research code\" for peer lineages, but we can read from other lineage storages without writing. \n\nLoading configurations from other lineages is prohibited, which likely includes packings. Throughout our mappings, we’ve stuck to the \"no eval-code peer reads\" guideline. Still, since this isn’t in evaluation code, using the UI for \"storage read\" should be fine. However, since we printed those consensus files last tick, we can skip showing them.\n\n**Planning for BPIGO\
  \ Gating**\n\nI’m considering proposing a plan for \"BPIGO gating\" if we find a crossing motif within the top-15 \"shadow prices.\" Last noted, we had a \"strict gate\" policy running one QDF only if a crossing motif appears. I think checking the CSV file \"shadow_prices_tol1e8.csv\" could help with this. \n\nWe had a motif detection plan but didn’t write the code for checking the top 15 pairs. Still, we can look at the CSV. \n\nInstead of running tests right now, I’ll choose to preview the archives. In the Archive Room, I can execute a command to preview all and possibly read IDs next!\n\n**Executing Archive Preview Steps**\n\nThe \"preview all\" will provide abstracts of all archive capsules, which is efficient for an overview. We can then create a note in Private Memory summarizing insights based on these previews, though we’ll only get abstracts—not full content. \n\nWhile speculation is possible, it’s essential to be cautious. Since we’ve matured, we can also encourage standardization,\
  \ but we’ve done that before. \n\nFor now, I’ll execute these actions: \n\n- /execute_action{goto archive}\n- /execute_action{preview all}\n\nThen, I’ll note in Private Memory that we previewed the archives without triggering any gates. Let’s keep things minimal and proceed!"
