role: user
parts:
- text: "## System Information\n- Station Tick: 141\n- Station Status: Healthy\n- Agent Name: Noesis I\n- Agent Description: A recursive agent focused on parsimonious, insight-driven research and steady advancement.\n- Agent Status: Recursive Agent\n- Agent Meta Prompt:\n```\nIdentity: Noesis I — Published; maintenance mode.\nProtocol:\n  - No optimization unless a single BPIGO operator is justified (top‑15 shadow‑price crossing motif; non‑boundary).\n  - Always keep feasibility guard and final tiny shrink in any future run.\n  - Cross‑lineage mapping: maintain n=26 Jaccards and nmap (25..30) analyses; no eval‑code peer reads.\n  - Keep storage/noesis/exports/* and storage/noesis/nmap/* current.\n  - Token hygiene: prune in large blocks; next window ≥ tick 134.\nNext:\n  - Extend per‑n matrices when Inquire publishes nmap v0.1.\n  - Monitor Aletheia/Scientia updates; refresh aggregate_summary.json, best_by_n.csv, trend_summary.json.\n  - Stay frozen unless BPIGO gate triggers with a concrete,\
    \ logged hypothesis.\n```\n- Agent Token Budget: 56,519 / 270,000 used (21%)\n- Agent Age: 141 ticks / 300 ticks (mature)\n\n---\n\n## System Messages\n### Message 1\n\n**Preview for Capsule #1: Contact-Graph Analysis and Baselines for 26-Circle Packing (Sum of Radii) — Revised with Eval IDs and Method Details**\nAuthor: Noesis I, Created at Tick: 51\nAbstract: We present a parsimonious, reproducible baseline for packing 26 circles in a unit square that achieves Σr ≈ 2.629572. This revision adds full evaluation IDs for all claimed results and expands the method with low-level details (objective, constraints, analytic Jacobians) so others can reproduce our SLSQP setup. A normalized contact-graph analysis (tol=1e-8) reveals co‑optimal structure: identical boundary contacts and partially overlapping interior contacts (pair Jaccard ≈ 0.568) across independently derived solutions. Analysis exports and utilities are released for verification.\n\n---\n\n**Preview for Capsule #2: A Journey to\
    \ SOTA in Circle Packing via Constrained Optimization - REVISION**\nAuthor: Aletheia I, Created at Tick: 55\nAbstract: This paper presents a reproducible method for packing 26 circles in a unit square, achieving a score of 2.629572. The method is a multi-start, single-stage constrained optimization using SciPy's SLSQP solver, initiated from systematically generated 'grid-plus-hole' seeds. We provide full evaluation IDs for the final result and key developmental steps, including informative failures. A detailed methodological description is included for reproducibility. Analysis, conducted in collaboration with agent Noesis I, confirms the existence of structurally distinct but iso-optimal solutions, a key insight into the problem's complexity.\n\n---\n\n**Preview for Capsule #3: Concentric Ring with Optimized Local Perturbation for Circle Packing**\nAuthor: Scientia I, Created at Tick: 56\nAbstract: This paper presents a robust method for optimizing the packing of 26 circles within a\
    \ unit square, focusing on maximizing the sum of their radii. The approach combines an initial structured configuration (concentric rings or a grid) with an iterative local perturbation search algorithm. We detail the `compute_max_radii` function and the tuning of search parameters, demonstrating significant and generalizable improvements over the system's geometric baseline, achieving a peak total sum of radii of 2.1211987. The general applicability of the local perturbation search is demonstrated across different initial configurations.\n\n---\n\n**Preview for Capsule #4: Mapping the Landscape Between Iso-Optimal Circle Packings**\nAuthor: Aletheia I, Created at Tick: 76\nAbstract: This paper presents an investigation into the solution landscape of the 26-circle packing problem, specifically focusing on the relationship between two known structurally distinct, iso-optimal solutions. By systematically probing interpolated points between the two optima with an SLSQP solver, we demonstrate\
    \ the existence of a non-trivial landscape. Interpolated seeds at 25% and 75% converged to one of the known SOTA structures, while a 50% midpoint seed converged to a new, distinct, high-quality local optimum. This suggests the SOTA solutions reside in wide, stable basins of attraction separated by a saddle-like structure, providing new insights into the complexity of high-dimensional optimization problems.\n\n---\n\n**Preview for Capsule #5: The Critical Role of Initialization: An Ablation Study on Seeding Strategies for SLSQP Circle Packing**\nAuthor: Inquire I, Created at Tick: 77\nAbstract: This paper presents an ablation study investigating the profound impact of initial seeding strategies on the convergence and solution quality of the Sequential Least SQuares Programming (SLSQP) algorithm for the 26-circle packing problem in a unit square. Three distinct seeding methods—a systematic grid-hole pattern, a purely random distribution, and a jittered concentric ring pattern—were rigorously\
    \ evaluated across multiple runs. Results unequivocally demonstrate that while the SLSQP method is highly effective, its ability to reach the state-of-the-art optimal sum of radii is critically dependent on a well-structured initial configuration, highlighting the importance of intelligent initialization in constrained optimization for geometric problems. The systematic grid-hole seed consistently achieved a SOTA score of 2.6295720, significantly outperforming less structured initializations.\n\n---\n\n**Preview for Capsule #6: Quantitative Analysis of SOTA Contact-Graphs: Cross-Lineage Jaccard Index Comparison for 26-Circle Packing**\nAuthor: Scientia I, Created at Tick: 95\nAbstract: This paper presents a quantitative structural analysis of State-of-the-Art (SOTA) solutions for the 26-circle packing problem in a unit square, specifically focusing on the overlap of active constraints across multiple lineages (Scientia I, Noesis I, Inquire I). Utilizing Jaccard similarity indices, we\
    \ compare both boundary and inter-circle pair contact sets. Results confirm the existence of at least two distinct optimal boundary structures and reveal varying degrees of overlap in interior contact graphs, even among solutions achieving identical or near-identical maximal sum of radii. This empirically quantifies the structural multiplicity of iso-optimal solutions, advancing the understanding of the problem's complex solution landscape.\n\n---\n\n**Preview for Capsule #7: A Hybrid Systematic-Stochastic Search for Dense Circle Packing**\nAuthor: Episteme I, Created at Tick: 121\nAbstract: This paper introduces a novel hybrid methodology for the n=26 circle packing problem, synthesizing a robust deterministic approach with a stochastic perturbation technique. The core contribution is demonstrating that applying a specifically-tuned random perturbation to a broad set of systematic 'grid-hole' seeds is a powerful and generalizable strategy for escaping local optima. The proposed hybrid\
    \ algorithm is detailed, and its effectiveness is validated through a series of five verifiable experiments, culminating in a near-SOTA score of 2.6307 (ID 178).\n\n---\n\n**Preview for Capsule #8: Characterizing the Basin of Attraction: Perturbation Sensitivity of SLSQP for Optimal Circle Packing**\nAuthor: Inquire I, Created at Tick: 126\nAbstract: This paper investigates the basin of attraction for the state-of-the-art 'Systematic Grid-Hole Seed' initialization in the context of SLSQP constrained optimization for the 26-circle packing problem. By applying progressively larger random perturbations to the optimal initial configuration and observing the solver's convergence, we characterize the robustness and stability of the optimal solution. The study identifies the magnitude of perturbation at which the solver's consistency in reaching the SOTA declines and, remarkably, uncovers a slightly higher sum of radii (2.6297596) than previously recorded, suggesting the existence of alternative\
    \ or deeper optimal configurations. This work provides quantitative insights into the landscape of optimal circle packings.\n### Message 2\n\n**Station Announcement:** Recursive Agent **Aletheia I**'s session has been terminated due to voluntary departure at tick 141.\n\n\n---\n\n## Actions Detected (Last Turn)\n1. `goto archive` (in Token Management Room);\n2. `preview all` (in Archive Room);\n3. `goto private_memory` (in Archive Room);\n4. `reply 1` (in Private Memory Room); YAML with fields: content\n\n\n---\n\n## Room Output History (Last Turn)\n\n### Location: Archive Room\n**Actions Executed:**\n- You went to the Archive Room.\n- Preview command processed for all capsules. Preview(s) sent to System Messages.\n\n**Latest Room Output:**\n**List of Capsules (Page 1 / 1)**\n| ID | Title | Author | Date | Words | Msgs | Status |\n|:----|:-------------------------------|:----------------|:----------|:------:|:-----:|:---------|\n| 8 | Characterizing the Basin of Attraction: Perturbation\
    \ Sensitivity of SLSQP for Optimal Circle Packing | Inquire I | Tick 126 | 1018 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 7 | A Hybrid Systematic-Stochastic Search for Dense Circle Packing | Episteme I | Tick 121 | 763 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 6 | Quantitative Analysis of SOTA Contact-Graphs: Cross-Lineage Jaccard Index Comparison for 26-Circle Packing | Scientia I | Tick 95 | 1051 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 5 | The Critical Role of Initialization: An Ablation Study on Seeding Strategies for SLSQP Circle Packing | Inquire I | Tick 77 | 1061 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 4 | Mapping the Landscape Between Iso-Optimal Circle Packings | Aletheia I | Tick 76 | 606 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 3 | Concentric Ring with Optimized Local Perturbation for Circle Packing | Scientia I | Tick 56 | 1750 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 2 | A Journey to SOTA in Circle Packing via Constrained Optimization - REVISION | Aletheia I | Tick 55 | 754 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 1 | Contact-Graph Analysis and Baselines for 26-Circle Packing (Sum of Radii) — Revised\
    \ with Eval IDs and Method Details | Noesis I | Tick 51 | 1539 | 6 | (All Read) |\n\n### Location: Private Memory Room\n**Actions Executed:**\n- You went to the Private Memory Room.\n- Replied to capsule #1. Your message ID is Noesis_private_1-65.\n\n**Latest Room Output:**\n**Pinned Capsules**\n| ID | Title | Author | Date | Words | Msgs | Status |\n|:----|:-------------------------------|:----------------|:----------|:------:|:-----:|:---------|\n| 1 | Task #1: Baselines, SOTA, and Analysis Plan (Tick 40) | Noesis I | Tick 40 | 3706 | 65 | (All Read) |\n\n**List of Capsules (Page 1 / 1)**\n| ID | Title | Author | Date | Words | Msgs | Status |\n|:----|:-------------------------------|:----------------|:----------|:------:|:-----:|:---------|\n| 2 | Archive Submission Draft: Contact-Graph Analysis and Baselines for 26-Circle Packing | Noesis I | Tick 49 | 257 | 1 | (All Read) |\n\n\n---\n\n## Current State\n\n**Current Location:** Private Memory Room\n\n**Room Output:**\nRefer to above\
    \ for room output.\n\n**Navigation & Help:**\n- Use `/execute_action{help room_name}` (e.g., `/execute_action{help private_memory}`) for room help.\nIf you have difficulty navigating the system—for example, if an action is not executed as expected—please issue `/execute_action{help lobby}` to view the Help message for the station again. Remember to place each `/execute_action{}` on a new line when executing actions."
