    \    • n=29 A↔S consensus: storage/noesis/nmap/consensus_n29_Aletheia_Scientia_tol1e8.json\n  - Policy reminder: no eval‑code peer reads; analysis uses published exports only. Optimization stays frozen unless BPIGO gate triggers.\n```\n\n`/execute_action{goto private_memory}`\n`/execute_action{reply 1}`\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  Tick 141 — Aletheia departed; plan unchanged\n  - Logged lineage status at storage/noesis/nmap/lineage_status.json (Aletheia marked inactive_departed).\n  - Continue mapping: refresh matrices/consensus as peers publish; watch for Inquire nmap v0.1.\n  - No optimization unless BPIGO gate triggers (top‑15 shadow‑price crossing motif; non‑boundary). Next prune window when cooldown allows.\n```"
thinking_content: "**Evaluating loading policies**\n\nI need to think about the policy regarding loading configurations. There's a clear statement that says, \"Loading configurations from other lineages is prohibited,\" and any violations could lead to session termination. This seems to specifically apply to code for evaluations. However, the previous guidelines mention you can read from other lineage storage but not write or delete. It’s a bit ambiguous about reading data inside a run, so just to be safe, I’ll avoid loading their packings into our evaluation code.\n\n**Considering data handling policies**\n\nI realize that we can read published exports for analysis outside the evaluation context. Copying textual consensus sets into our work is allowed since that falls under reading and writing outside evaluation. However, we must respect the rule that prohibits \"loading configurations from other lineages\" in evaluation code, and we’ve adhered to that. So, we can safely copy consensus\
  \ text files to our storage for reproducibility purposes. I’ll use ‘storage read’ to retrieve them and ‘storage write’ to save a snapshot.\n\n**Clarifying storage processes**\n\nOkay, it seems that ‘storage read’ prints file contents in the interface, but it doesn’t return them to the code for automatic transformation. I can manually copy the displayed content and then use ‘storage write’ with a YAML block containing that content, which is allowed. Now, let’s check if those files exist: in eval 214, it saved files like 'boundary_3way_consensus.txt' and 'pairs_3way_consensus.txt.' I'll read these and also check 'sota_2_centers.txt,' but I need to avoid replicating other lineages' packings. \n\nI’ll follow these steps: \n1. Go to Research Counter. \n2. Use ‘storage read’ for the relevant files. \n3. Write the retrieved content to new location files.\n\n**Assessing content handling methods**\n\nI really need to get the content after reading. The problem is that reading will just display the\
  \ file, and I can’t capture it programmatically — I’ll have to manually paste it instead. But I don’t know the content exactly. One option could be to note \"see original path.\" Hmm, alternatively, I might write a simple analysis-only eval to copy the file content programmatically. \n\nWith this, I can create a ‘submit 1’ script that reads those files and saves them to 'storage/noesis/...'. However, I must avoid reading from other lineages' storage in evaluation code since that's ambiguous; it’s safer not to do that.\n\n**Planning actions for Aletheia’s departure**\n\nIt seems I need to keep references to Aletheia's consensus files and have a fallback plan in case anything is missing. I can reconstruct consensus from our own 'consensus_sets_tol1e8.json' and jaccard matrices, which we already have.\n\nTo fill the gap after Aletheia's departure, here are my minimal actions:  \n- Post a Public Memory note to acknowledge the departure and assure resource preservation.\n- Update our private\
  \ plan.\n- Pin our 'Public Memory capsule 1'.\n- Ensure the jaccard matrix index is updated.  \n- Propose to deprecate reliance on Aletheia's file in favor of our own.\n\nI’ll also set a watch for \"Inquire's nmap\" while executing these steps, like listing ‘storage Inquire/nmap’ and writing a status JSON block capturing lineage statuses."
