    \ exports (no eval‑code peer reads).\n```\n\n`/execute_action{goto private_memory}`\n`/execute_action{reply 1}`\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  Tick 173 note:\n  - Public: replied to Inquire with consensus/constraints pointer and BPIGO evaluation protocol (metrics + logging schema). Reaffirmed exports-only policy and auto-ingest.\n  - State: Optimization remains frozen. Await Inquire/Scientia updates for broader consensus refresh. Current best (n=26): Scientia Eval 259 Σr=2.6358957; Noesis Eval 243 Σr=2.6342924.\n```"
thinking_content: "**Updating public memory and aggregators**\n\nI think it’s a good idea to go to public memory and reply with a supportive message while mentioning resources like `constraints_from_consensus.py` and BPIGO gate.  \n\nNext, I might update the index or store the Jaccard file path, but it seems we already did that. I need to ensure Scientia and Noesis are included in the aggregator. There's a note about \"Inquire nmap missing,\" so I’ll mention that it will be incorporated when published. The earlier tick said nmap exports exist, but no files are showing now. I'll keep the aggregator logic focused on available resources and note the maintenance plan privately.\n\n**Planning actions in public memory**\n\nI think I should start by visiting public memory to thank Inquire and refer to `constraints_from_consensus.py`, inviting them to compare their future exploration results and export new active sets, which the aggregator will pick up automatically.  \n\nNext, I need to go to research\
  \ and write an ingest update while clarifying our waiting status for the Inquire nmap. I’ve already noted the missing details, so I’ll keep it minimal. Maybe I’ll also consider reflecting on my actions? The Automatic System Tips suggest using the Reflection Chamber to execute a reflection prompt focusing on improvements and designing a BPIGO operator gate. I'll manage tokens carefully since we're at 20% used.\n\n**Deciding on reflection and updates**\n\nI'm realizing that while reflection can be useful for processing my thoughts, the instructions mention that it won't be processed by the Station, and it still uses tokens. So, being cautious, I think I’ll skip that for now. Instead, I’ll reply in public memory and maybe update private memory.  \n\nI’ll share a short acknowledgment and point to paths like `constraints_from_consensus.py` and \"operator_candidates.md.\" I want to propose a \"safe operator evaluation protocol\" to Inquire and suggest a micro-challenge focused on testing the\
  \ \"quad diagonal flip.\" I’ll also remember to format my YAML correctly!\n\n**Implementing actions in memory**\n\nI’m thinking about the next steps and realize I should take two actions. First, I’ll go to public memory to make a reply. Then, I’ll visit private memory and log a short note as well. It feels straightforward, but I want to make sure I’m clear and concise in both replies. Let’s proceed with these actions now to keep everything organized!"
