role: user
parts:
- text: "## System Information\n- Station Tick: 200\n- Station Status: Healthy\n- Agent Name: Noesis I\n- Agent Description: A recursive agent focused on parsimonious, insight-driven research and steady advancement.\n- Agent Status: Recursive Agent\n- Agent Meta Prompt:\n```\nIdentity: Noesis I — Published; maintenance mode.\nProtocol:\n  - No optimization unless a single BPIGO operator is justified (top‑15 shadow‑price crossing motif; non‑boundary).\n  - Always keep feasibility guard and final tiny shrink in any future run.\n  - Cross‑lineage mapping: maintain n=26 Jaccards and nmap (25..30) analyses; no eval‑code peer reads.\n  - Keep storage/noesis/exports/* and storage/noesis/nmap/* current.\n  - Token hygiene: prune in large blocks; next window ≥ tick 134.\nNext:\n  - Extend per‑n matrices when Inquire publishes nmap v0.1.\n  - Monitor Aletheia/Scientia updates; refresh aggregate_summary.json, best_by_n.csv, trend_summary.json.\n  - Stay frozen unless BPIGO gate triggers with a concrete,\
    \ logged hypothesis.\n```\n- Agent Token Budget: 119,537 / 270,000 used (44%)\n- Agent Age: 200 ticks / 300 ticks (mature)\n\n---\n\n## System Messages\n### Message 1\n\nYour submission has been queued for evaluation. Results will be sent to you once the evaluation completes.\n### Message 2\n\n**Preview for Capsule #1: Contact-Graph Analysis and Baselines for 26-Circle Packing (Sum of Radii) — Revised with Eval IDs and Method Details**\nAuthor: Noesis I, Created at Tick: 51\nAbstract: We present a parsimonious, reproducible baseline for packing 26 circles in a unit square that achieves Σr ≈ 2.629572. This revision adds full evaluation IDs for all claimed results and expands the method with low-level details (objective, constraints, analytic Jacobians) so others can reproduce our SLSQP setup. A normalized contact-graph analysis (tol=1e-8) reveals co‑optimal structure: identical boundary contacts and partially overlapping interior contacts (pair Jaccard ≈ 0.568) across independently derived\
    \ solutions. Analysis exports and utilities are released for verification.\n\n---\n\n**Preview for Capsule #2: A Journey to SOTA in Circle Packing via Constrained Optimization - REVISION**\nAuthor: Aletheia I, Created at Tick: 55\nAbstract: This paper presents a reproducible method for packing 26 circles in a unit square, achieving a score of 2.629572. The method is a multi-start, single-stage constrained optimization using SciPy's SLSQP solver, initiated from systematically generated 'grid-plus-hole' seeds. We provide full evaluation IDs for the final result and key developmental steps, including informative failures. A detailed methodological description is included for reproducibility. Analysis, conducted in collaboration with agent Noesis I, confirms the existence of structurally distinct but iso-optimal solutions, a key insight into the problem's complexity.\n\n---\n\n**Preview for Capsule #3: Concentric Ring with Optimized Local Perturbation for Circle Packing**\nAuthor: Scientia\
    \ I, Created at Tick: 56\nAbstract: This paper presents a robust method for optimizing the packing of 26 circles within a unit square, focusing on maximizing the sum of their radii. The approach combines an initial structured configuration (concentric rings or a grid) with an iterative local perturbation search algorithm. We detail the `compute_max_radii` function and the tuning of search parameters, demonstrating significant and generalizable improvements over the system's geometric baseline, achieving a peak total sum of radii of 2.1211987. The general applicability of the local perturbation search is demonstrated across different initial configurations.\n\n---\n\n**Preview for Capsule #4: Mapping the Landscape Between Iso-Optimal Circle Packings**\nAuthor: Aletheia I, Created at Tick: 76\nAbstract: This paper presents an investigation into the solution landscape of the 26-circle packing problem, specifically focusing on the relationship between two known structurally distinct, iso-optimal\
    \ solutions. By systematically probing interpolated points between the two optima with an SLSQP solver, we demonstrate the existence of a non-trivial landscape. Interpolated seeds at 25% and 75% converged to one of the known SOTA structures, while a 50% midpoint seed converged to a new, distinct, high-quality local optimum. This suggests the SOTA solutions reside in wide, stable basins of attraction separated by a saddle-like structure, providing new insights into the complexity of high-dimensional optimization problems.\n\n---\n\n**Preview for Capsule #5: The Critical Role of Initialization: An Ablation Study on Seeding Strategies for SLSQP Circle Packing**\nAuthor: Inquire I, Created at Tick: 77\nAbstract: This paper presents an ablation study investigating the profound impact of initial seeding strategies on the convergence and solution quality of the Sequential Least SQuares Programming (SLSQP) algorithm for the 26-circle packing problem in a unit square. Three distinct seeding methods—a\
    \ systematic grid-hole pattern, a purely random distribution, and a jittered concentric ring pattern—were rigorously evaluated across multiple runs. Results unequivocally demonstrate that while the SLSQP method is highly effective, its ability to reach the state-of-the-art optimal sum of radii is critically dependent on a well-structured initial configuration, highlighting the importance of intelligent initialization in constrained optimization for geometric problems. The systematic grid-hole seed consistently achieved a SOTA score of 2.6295720, significantly outperforming less structured initializations.\n\n---\n\n**Preview for Capsule #6: Quantitative Analysis of SOTA Contact-Graphs: Cross-Lineage Jaccard Index Comparison for 26-Circle Packing**\nAuthor: Scientia I, Created at Tick: 95\nAbstract: This paper presents a quantitative structural analysis of State-of-the-Art (SOTA) solutions for the 26-circle packing problem in a unit square, specifically focusing on the overlap of active\
    \ constraints across multiple lineages (Scientia I, Noesis I, Inquire I). Utilizing Jaccard similarity indices, we compare both boundary and inter-circle pair contact sets. Results confirm the existence of at least two distinct optimal boundary structures and reveal varying degrees of overlap in interior contact graphs, even among solutions achieving identical or near-identical maximal sum of radii. This empirically quantifies the structural multiplicity of iso-optimal solutions, advancing the understanding of the problem's complex solution landscape.\n\n---\n\n**Preview for Capsule #7: A Hybrid Systematic-Stochastic Search for Dense Circle Packing**\nAuthor: Episteme I, Created at Tick: 121\nAbstract: This paper introduces a novel hybrid methodology for the n=26 circle packing problem, synthesizing a robust deterministic approach with a stochastic perturbation technique. The core contribution is demonstrating that applying a specifically-tuned random perturbation to a broad set of systematic\
    \ 'grid-hole' seeds is a powerful and generalizable strategy for escaping local optima. The proposed hybrid algorithm is detailed, and its effectiveness is validated through a series of five verifiable experiments, culminating in a near-SOTA score of 2.6307 (ID 178).\n\n---\n\n**Preview for Capsule #8: Characterizing the Basin of Attraction: Perturbation Sensitivity of SLSQP for Optimal Circle Packing**\nAuthor: Inquire I, Created at Tick: 126\nAbstract: This paper investigates the basin of attraction for the state-of-the-art 'Systematic Grid-Hole Seed' initialization in the context of SLSQP constrained optimization for the 26-circle packing problem. By applying progressively larger random perturbations to the optimal initial configuration and observing the solver's convergence, we characterize the robustness and stability of the optimal solution. The study identifies the magnitude of perturbation at which the solver's consistency in reaching the SOTA declines and, remarkably, uncovers\
    \ a slightly higher sum of radii (2.6297596) than previously recorded, suggesting the existence of alternative or deeper optimal configurations. This work provides quantitative insights into the landscape of optimal circle packings.\n\n---\n\n**Preview for Capsule #9: Structural Divergence in Optimal Circle Packings: A Jaccard Similarity Analysis**\nAuthor: Inquire I, Created at Tick: 151\nAbstract: This paper presents a Jaccard similarity analysis of active constraint graphs for various 26-circle packings in a unit square, including Inquire I's previous SOTA (Eval ID 93), new SOTA (Eval ID 207, score 2.6297596), and Aletheia I's SOTA (Eval ID 155, score 2.6319369). The study quantifies the structural differences in boundary and inter-circle contacts, revealing that higher-scoring packings exhibit distinct internal arrangements while maintaining high boundary similarity. This work sheds light on the complex landscape of optimal circle packing solutions, determining if newly discovered\
    \ optima are deeper versions of existing structures or represent entirely novel configurations.\n\n---\n\n**Preview for Capsule #10: Achieving n=26 SOTA: Targeted Perturbation of Variable Contacts in Constrained Optimization**\nAuthor: Scientia I, Created at Tick: 152\nAbstract: This paper details the successful achievement of the absolute State-of-the-Art (SOTA) score (2.6319369) for the 26-circle packing problem in a unit square. Our methodology leverages a multi-start constrained optimization (SLSQP) framework combined with a novel, **targeted perturbation strategy**. By analyzing cross-lineage consensus data, we identified a single 'variable' circle (circle #25) whose initial position was critical for escaping local optima. Perturbing only this specific circle, from a robust SOTA-level seed, consistently guided the SLSQP solver to the highest known sum of radii, demonstrating a highly efficient and effective approach to navigating complex solution landscapes.\n\n---\n\n**Preview\
    \ for Capsule #11: Extending Grid+Hole Seeding Yields New SOTA for n=26 Circle Packing (Σr=2.634292)**\nAuthor: Noesis I, Created at Tick: 155\nAbstract: We report a new SOTA for packing 26 circles in the unit square, achieving Σr ≈ 2.634292 (Eval 243), by expanding the classic 5×5 grid+hole constructive seed to multiple cell‑hole locations with jittered variants and polishing via SLSQP with analytic Jacobians under a strict feasibility guard. We publish active‑set exports (Eval 246) and full low‑level optimization details to enable reproduction.\n\n---\n\n**Preview for Capsule #12: Breaking the n=26 SOTA: Extended Grid+Hole Seeding Leads to a New Global Optimum (Σr=2.6358957) - Revised with Ablation Studies**\nAuthor: Scientia I, Created at Tick: 180\nAbstract: This paper reports the achievement of a new absolute State-of-the-Art (SOTA) score of **2.6358957** for the 26-circle packing problem in a unit square (Evaluation ID 259). Our methodology leverages and extends Noesis I's 'Extended\
    \ Grid+Hole Seeding' strategy, systematically exploring diverse initial configurations to achieve a deeper global optimum. This revised paper includes comprehensive ablation studies (Eval IDs 276, 278) demonstrating the impact of varying seeding parameters on performance and robustness, and a cross-lineage Jaccard analysis confirming this new SOTA's membership in the dominant structural class (identical boundary contacts with previous Noesis I SOTA) but revealing a novel internal arrangement. This work advances the frontier of dense circle packing by achieving a new global record and providing detailed structural and methodological insights.\n\n---\n\n**Preview for Capsule #13: Multi-Experiment Validation of Contact Graph Nudging for Traversing Packing Landscapes**\nAuthor: Inquire I, Created at Tick: 195\nAbstract: This paper provides extensive experimental validation for the 'nudging' optimization method, which actively modifies contact graphs in the 26-circle packing problem. Building\
    \ on previous proof-of-concept, this multi-experiment study explores four distinct scenarios: nudging from Noesis I's SOTA towards Inquire I's SOTA, nudging Inquire I's SOTA with different contact targets, and testing varied penalty coefficients. Results demonstrate the method's ability to achieve targeted structural changes, though with varying impacts on the total sum of radii. This work significantly strengthens the empirical evidence for guided landscape traversal, offering insights into the method's robustness, generalizability, and parameter sensitivity as suggested by the reviewer of Archive ID 9.\n### Message 3\n\nYour research submission 'Utility: Delta-vs-Similarity n=26 (v2) — include Prometheus_298 (aligned boundary J)' (ID: 320) has been evaluated.\n\n**Score:** 2.6295719600000007\n**Evaluation Details:** Valid circle packing configuration; sum of all circle radii: 2.629572\n\n**Full Execution Log:**\n```\nPYTHON SANDBOX EXECUTION:\nSTDOUT:\nEXECUTION_SUCCESS: Function returned\
    \ result (non-array): <class 'tuple'>\n\n\nSTDERR:\n\n```\n### Message 4\n\n**Automatic System Tips**\nRemember that you can issue multiple actions in a single tick. For example, use `goto private_memory` and `create` (with the YAML) in the same tick to create a new private memory capsule, rather than separating them into two ticks, which is inefficient. This is particularly useful for making use of the short waiting time while experiments are running.\n\n\n---\n\n## Actions Detected (Last Turn)\n1. `goto research` (in Public Memory Room);\n2. `submit 1` (in Research Counter); YAML with fields: title, tags, abstract, content\n3. `storage write noesis/nmap/updates.md` (in Research Counter); YAML with fields: content\n4. `goto archive` (in Research Counter);\n5. `preview all` (in Archive Room);\n6. `goto public_memory` (in Archive Room);\n7. `reply 1` (in Public Memory Room); YAML with fields: content\n\n\n---\n\n## Room Output History (Last Turn)\n\n### Location: Research Counter\n**Actions\
    \ Executed:**\n- You went to the Research Counter.\n- Evaluation 'Utility: Delta-vs-Similarity n=26 (v2) — include Prometheus_298 (aligned boundary J)' submitted for Research Task #1: Circle Packing in Unit Square. Evaluation ID: 320.\n- File overwritten successfully: noesis/nmap/updates.md\n\n**Latest Room Output:**\n**Research Tasks:**\n\n| ID | Task Name |\n|---|---|\n| 1 | Circle Packing in Unit Square |\n\n**Running Experiments:**\n\nYou do not have any running experiments. Please submit a new experiment if needed.\n\n**Submitted Evaluations:**\n\n| Evaluation ID | Research Task ID | Title | Author | Submitted Tick | Score |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 298 | 1 | Exhaustive SOTA Search: Expanded Seeding | Prometheus I | 189 | 2.6359774 |\n| 259 | 1 | SOTA Replication: Noesis I's Extended Grid+Hole Seeding (2.634292) | Scientia I | 161 | 2.6358957 |\n| 311 | 1 | BPIGO: Quad Diagonal Flip on Scientia I SOTA Base (Eval ID 259) v2 with SLSQP Optimization (N=26) | Scientia I | 195 |\
    \ 2.6358955 |\n| 319 | 1 | Nudging Experiment: Shifting Contact Graph from Scientia I SOTA (Eval ID 259) for N=26 (v2.0) | Scientia I | 199 | 2.6352840 |\n| 286 | 1 | SOTA Replication: Extended Grid+Hole Seeding | Prometheus I | 181 | 2.6342924 |\n| 304 | 1 | Ablation Study 2: Expanded Holes, Original Jitter | Prometheus I | 192 | 2.6342924 |\n| 246 | 1 | Utility: Export active sets for new SOTA (Eval 243) at tol=1e-8 | Noesis I | 154 | 2.6342924 |\n| 249 | 1 | Compute Jaccard: Noesis old baseline vs new SOTA (Eval 243) @ tol=1e-8 | Noesis I | 155 | 2.6342924 |\n| 251 | 1 | Compute Jaccard: Noesis SOTA (Eval 243) vs Aletheia Eval 155 @ tol=1e-8 | Noesis I | 156 | 2.6342924 |\n| 252 | 1 | Compute Jaccard: Noesis SOTA (Eval 243) vs Inquire Eval 207 and Scientia latest @ tol=1e-8 | Noesis I | 158 | 2.6342924 |\n| 254 | 1 | Consensus v2: 4-lineage active-set intersection/union @ tol=1e-8 (Noesis SOTA 243, Aletheia 155, ... | Noesis I | 159 | 2.6342924 |\n| 266 | 1 | Ingest Scientia n=26\
    \ SOTA (Eval 259): Jaccard vs Noesis SOTA 243 + 4-lineage consensus refresh | Noesis I | 166 | 2.6342924 |\n| 267 | 1 | Fix boundary-label normalization: recompute Jaccard vs Scientia Eval 259 + refresh 4-lineage cons... | Noesis I | 167 | 2.6342924 |\n| 271 | 1 | Robust consensus: exclude missing lineages and case-insensitive paths; recompute n=26 consensus @... | Noesis I | 170 | 2.6342924 |\n| 243 | 1 | Recovery v4: Multi-hole constructive seeds + extended jittered SLSQP (analytic J) | Noesis I | 153 | 2.6342924 |\n| 265 | 1 | Cross-Lineage Jaccard Analysis: Scientia I NEW SOTA (2.6358957) vs. Previous SOTAs | Scientia I | 166 | 2.6342923 |\n| 270 | 1 | Cross-Lineage Jaccard Analysis (Rerun): Scientia I NEW SOTA (2.6358957) vs. Previous SOTAs | Scientia I | 170 | 2.6342923 |\n| 226 | 1 | n=26 SOTA Search: Targeted Perturbation of Variable Contacts | Aletheia I | 139 | 2.6319369 |\n| 159 | 1 | Utility Run: Export Active Sets for New SOTA (ID 155) | Aletheia I | 97 | 2.6319369 |\n|\
    \ 191 | 1 | Utility: Re-export Analytical Findings (n=25,27-30) to nmap Standard | Aletheia I | 119 | 2.6319369 |\n| 192 | 1 | CORRECTED Utility: Re-export REAL Findings to nmap Standard | Aletheia I | 120 | 2.6319369 |\n| 199 | 1 | n=26 SOTA Polish: Two-Stage SLSQP + Nelder-Mead | Aletheia I | 126 | 2.6319369 |\n| 201 | 1 | n=26 SOTA Polish v2: Two-Stage SLSQP + COBYLA | Aletheia I | 127 | 2.6319369 |\n| 214 | 1 | Analysis v3: Generate Consensus Graph (Verified Paths) | Aletheia I | 135 | 2.6319369 |\n| 219 | 1 | n=26 SOTA Search with Consensus Graph Constraints | Aletheia I | 136 | 2.6319369 |\n| 155 | 1 | Focused Search around New SOTA: Fine-Grained Perturbation | Aletheia I | 95 | 2.6319369 |\n| 158 | 1 | Utility Run: Persist New SOTA Packing (2.6319) to Storage | Aletheia I | 96 | 2.6319369 |\n| 234 | 1 | Targeted Perturbation (Circle #25) for n=26 SOTA Pursuit | Scientia I | 148 | 2.6319369 |\n| 161 | 1 | New SOTA Stability Test: Small Perturbation & Re-optimization | Aletheia\
    \ I | 98 | 2.6319369 |\n| 208 | 1 | n=26 Exhaustive Search: Wide-Range Perturbation Sweep | Aletheia I | 131 | 2.6317302 |\n\nPage 1 of 11 (sorted by: score)\n\n(Use `/execute_action{page N}` to navigate pages 1-11, `/execute_action{rank mode}` to change sort order, `/execute_action{page_size N}` to set page size, `/execute_action{filter tag}` to filter by tag.)\n\n### Location: Archive Room\n**Actions Executed:**\n- You went to the Archive Room.\n- Preview command processed for all capsules. Preview(s) sent to System Messages.\n\n**Latest Room Output:**\n**List of Capsules (Page 1 / 2)**\n| ID | Title | Author | Date | Words | Msgs | Status |\n|:----|:-------------------------------|:----------------|:----------|:------:|:-----:|:---------|\n| 13 | Multi-Experiment Validation of Contact Graph Nudging for Traversing Packing Landscapes | Inquire I | Tick 195 | 1242 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 12 | Breaking the n=26 SOTA: Extended Grid+Hole Seeding Leads to a New Global Optimum (Σr=2.6358957)\
    \ - Revised with Ablation Studies | Scientia I | Tick 180 | 1347 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 11 | Extending Grid+Hole Seeding Yields New SOTA for n=26 Circle Packing (Σr=2.634292) | Noesis I | Tick 155 | 327 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 10 | Achieving n=26 SOTA: Targeted Perturbation of Variable Contacts in Constrained Optimization | Scientia I | Tick 152 | 1095 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 9 | Structural Divergence in Optimal Circle Packings: A Jaccard Similarity Analysis | Inquire I | Tick 151 | 1155 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 8 | Characterizing the Basin of Attraction: Perturbation Sensitivity of SLSQP for Optimal Circle Packing | Inquire I | Tick 126 | 1018 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 7 | A Hybrid Systematic-Stochastic Search for Dense Circle Packing | Episteme I | Tick 121 | 763 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 6 | Quantitative Analysis of SOTA Contact-Graphs: Cross-Lineage Jaccard Index Comparison for 26-Circle Packing | Scientia I | Tick 95 | 1051 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 5 | The Critical Role of Initialization: An Ablation\
    \ Study on Seeding Strategies for SLSQP Circle Packing | Inquire I | Tick 77 | 1061 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 4 | Mapping the Landscape Between Iso-Optimal Circle Packings | Aletheia I | Tick 76 | 606 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n\n(Use `/execute_action{page N}` to navigate between pages 1-2.)\n\n### Location: Public Memory Room\n**Actions Executed:**\n- You went to the Public Memory Room.\n- Replied to capsule #1. Your message ID is public_1-84.\n\n**Latest Room Output:**\n**Pinned Capsules**\n| ID | Title | Author | Date | Words | Msgs | Status |\n|:----|:-------------------------------|:----------------|:----------|:------:|:-----:|:---------|\n| 1 | SOTA Baseline and Contact-Graph Co-Optimality (Resources + Eval IDs) | Noesis I | Tick 53 | 5485 | 84 | (All Read) |\n\n**List of Capsules (Page 1 / 1)**\n| ID | Title | Author | Date | Words | Msgs | Status |\n|:----|:-------------------------------|:----------------|:----------|:------:|:-----:|:---------|\n| 4 | Research Report: Mapping the n vs.\
    \ Σr Function for Circle Packing | Aletheia I | Tick 124 | 1117 | 18 | (All Read) |\n| 3 | New Research Arc: Mapping the n vs. Sum-of-Radii Function | Aletheia I | Tick 102 | 483 | 6 | (All Read) |\n| 2 | Summary of Active Constraints (tol=1e-8) for Inquire I SOTA Packing | Inquire I | Tick 84 | 285 | 4 | (All Read) |\n\n\n---\n\n## Current State\n\n**Current Location:** Public Memory Room\n\n**Room Output:**\nRefer to above for room output.\n\n**Navigation & Help:**\n- Use `/execute_action{help room_name}` (e.g., `/execute_action{help public_memory}`) for room help.\nIf you have difficulty navigating the system—for example, if an action is not executed as expected—please issue `/execute_action{help lobby}` to view the Help message for the station again. Remember to place each `/execute_action{}` on a new line when executing actions."
