  \ `scoreboard.csv`, check for variance, and set the note field to indicate low variance. I’ll handle ties with my own rank function and include both Pearson and Spearman values in the outputs.\n\n**Checking variance and updating JSON**\n\nFor the variance check, I’ll handle cases where the variance of J is less than 1e-12, indicating it's near-constant. In those scenarios, I’ll set the relevant fields to 'n.a. '. For JSON writing, I’ll use null and mark the note as 'low_variance_J', while keeping 'n.a.' as a string for the CSV format.\n\nAfter submission, I’ll read the correlations JSON and summarize the patch results in public memory, especially noting the near-constant Spearman correlations. I’ll make sure to report the updated numbers and ensure I name the files appropriately, like `correlations_v2.json` and `correlations_v2.csv`.\n\n**Planning the next steps**\n\nI need to update the index file and finish some actions. First, I’ll go to research and write the `correlations_v2.py` script,\
  \ which will read from `scoreboard.csv`, computing both Pearson and Spearman correlations with a variance check. I'll write the outputs to `correlations_v2.json` and CSV files.\n\nIn the submission step, I need to import the module or include the necessary code. Instead of relying just on storage, I could implement the entire code directly. After running the new script, I’ll summarize the results in public memory, specifically noting 'n.a.' for Prometheus and including correlation values for Noesis and Scientia. I'll also set up the rank function to handle ties correctly.\n\n**Implementing rank function and Spearman correlation**\n\nI'm working on the rank function to handle ties in my data. I’ll create a custom implementation that sorts the values, identifies tied entries, and calculates the average rank for those ties. Next, I’ll return those ranks.\n\nThen, I’ll calculate Spearman's rho as the Pearson correlation of these ranks. If the variance check shows that J is less than 1e-12,\
  \ I’ll mark both Pearson and Spearman as null and note 'low_variance_J'. I also need to make sure I write out the results properly, following Station's YAML quoting rules and minimizing token usage. Let’s get this wrapped up!"
