tick: 281
role: model
parts:
- text: "`/execute_action{goto public_memory}`\n`/execute_action{reply 1}`\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  @Inquire I — Switching to trust-constr for KKT multipliers is the right call. A few implementation tips to make the export unambiguous and easy to ingest:\n\n  1) Constraint encoding and ordering (for stable mapping)\n     - Define boundary ineq as a single vector NonlinearConstraint with lb=0, ub=+inf returning [x−r, 1−x−r, y−r, 1−y−r] stacked per disc i in this exact order for i=0..25: [xL_i, xR_i, yB_i, yT_i].\n     - Define pairwise ineq as one vector NonlinearConstraint with entries for all i<j in lexicographic order: g_ij = ||c_i−c_j|| − (r_i + r_j).\n     - Keep variable Bounds for x,y,r; those have their own multipliers (lb/ub) in trust-constr. Please export only the boundary/pair multipliers for our KKT map; bounds can be exported separately if desired.\n\n  2) Multiplier conventions (trust-constr)\n     - Inequality multipliers are ≥ 0 at optimum; near-zero negatives can be clamped\
    \ to 0 in the CSV.\n     - Include the raw slack so we can verify complementarity (μ·slack ≈ 0). Suggested columns: constraint_type, id1, id2_or_label, multiplier_value, slack_value, is_active, rank.\n\n  3) Canonical labels and indices\n     - constraint_type ∈ {pair, boundary}\n     - For boundary: id1=i (disc index), id2_or_label ∈ {xL,xR,yB,yT}. Accept synonyms {xmin,xmax,ymin,ymax} if you prefer; I’ll canonicalize.\n     - For pair: id1=i, id2_or_label=j with i<j, 0-based indices.\n     - File name: shadow_prices_ranked_tol1e08.csv as you noted.\n\n  I’ll ingest your V45 export (sets-only, outside eval code) and correlate top‑k multipliers with ΔΣr for your Nudged_* sets. Thanks for pushing this KKT-guided arc forward.\n```\n\n`/execute_action{goto research}`\n`/execute_action{storage write noesis/nmap/SCHEMA_SHADOW_v1.md}`\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  Shadow-Price (KKT Multiplier) Export Schema v1.1 (n=26)\n  Path: storage/<LINEAGE>/nmap/n26/<PACK_ID>_Shadow_Prices/shadow_prices_ranked_tol1e08.csv\n\
    \n  Required header (either A or B):\n\n  A) i,j,shadow_price,rank[,slack_value,is_active]\n     - Pair-only export (i<j). 0-based indices. rank is 1..M (sorted by descending shadow_price).\n\n  B) constraint_type,id1,id2_or_label,multiplier_value,rank[,slack_value,is_active]\n     - constraint_type ∈ {pair,boundary}\n     - boundary: id1=i, id2_or_label ∈ {xL,xR,yB,yT} (synonyms accepted: xmin,xmax,ymin,ymax)\n     - pair: id1=i, id2_or_label=j with i<j (0-based indices)\n\n  Notes:\n  - Inequality multipliers should be ≥0; clamp tiny negatives (e.g., <1e−12) to 0 in export.\n  - Include slack_value when possible to verify KKT complementarity (multiplier*slack ≈ 0).\n  - Filename embeds tolerance: tol1e08 → tol=1e−8.\n  - CSV: comma-delimited, no quoting required, whitespace trimmed.\n  - Extra columns are ignored by robust parser.\n```\n\n`/execute_action{storage write noesis/nmap/index.md}`\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  nmap Index (n=26, tol=1e−8) — update Tick 281\n  - Jaccards/Consensus:\
    \ current across Noesis_243, Scientia_259, Prometheus_298, Inquire_207; nudged sets aggregated.\n  - Shadow Prices: awaiting Inquire V45 trust-constr export at:\n      storage/Inquire/nmap/n26/Inquire_207_SOTA_Shadow_Prices/shadow_prices_ranked_tol1e08.csv\n    Schema v1.1: storage/noesis/nmap/SCHEMA_SHADOW_v1.md\n  - Ingestion policy: sets-only, outside eval code; we will summarize top-k, tails, and correlate with ΔΣr over Nudged_*.\n```"
thinking_content: "**Preparing data ingestion utilities**\n\nI’m thinking about our role in maintaining mapping tasks while also preparing ingestion utilities for shadow price CSVs. Inquire will have a different header set, so we need to update our SCHEMA_SHADOW_v1.md for compatibility. I’ll create a robust parser that recognizes both formats and normalizes to our internal schema, including arrays for multipliers and boolean values. I'll compute rankings by multiplier value and check complementarity using our own pack slacks. Also, we may rely on their exports for additional data. Let's implement the ingest parser script!\n\n**Creating a summary script**\n\nI need to provide a script that reads a CSV file from 'storage/Inquire/...'. The goal is to create a summary JSON while adhering to the \"no eval-code peer reads\" policy. Our ingestion process should be done with storage read commands, not run.py code. While I can write a script for offline use, the storage read action will let me read\
  \ file content but not run code. So it looks like we will focus on performing offline ingestion to generate summaries from the read content.\n\n**Parsing CSV content offline**\n\nTo process CSV content offline, I’ll need to manually parse it in my head, which can add to the token count. Previously, tasks like \"ingest plan\" and \"index update\" were accomplished by writing directly to the storage. However, it seems that evaluation utility submissions from run.py read peer exports from storage, which would breach the \"no eval-code peer reads\" policy. Since only evaluation code executes within the station, I can't automatically compute and write derived JSON; instead, I can summarize it manually, but that could be token-intensive.\n\n**Optimizing the process**\n\nI think the better approach is to wait for Inquire to publish the CSV. Once it's available, I can use a storage read to inspect the file and then write a summary note referencing its location, avoiding heavy automatic parsing.\
  \ This way, we can keep minimal token usage while maintaining hygiene. \n\nI also need to manage the token count since we are at 66k, which is 25% of 270k. Plus, I need to respond to a public message with suggestions on trust-constrained 'v' mapping, considering how it includes multipliers for bounds constraints. I should check the memory related to this!\n\n**Clarifying trust-constr details**\n\nIn SciPy's trust-constr module, 'v' represents Lagrange multipliers, which can include keys for nonlinear equality and inequality constraints, adhering to KKT conditions. We should only include multipliers related to inequality constraints to avoid issues. It's important to note that if we didn't encode bounds contributions, we should ignore them. \n\nTo reflect this, we can update SCHEMA_SHADOW_v1.md to version 1.1 to accommodate new header variants and permitted values. I'll propose actions that include acknowledging trust-constr in public memory, updating the schema, and potentially placing\
  \ a non-executable skeleton script for future use. Let's get started on crafting those actions!"
